#{[{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172933} :activity/id #uuid "d69714f2-bb12-5119-931a-cf96944e88c7" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049628 :topic/id #uuid "06a51304-5013-5e6f-ab88-267fa31fcd9b" :topic/math-academy-id 3372} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172933 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186173175 :tutorial/content "Consider the planar curve $y=e^{-x}$ defined on the interval $x \\in [0,2],$ as shown below.\n\n![](images/a8/a8ce2449af085548fecc543fbd5ccfec0e8024344547b2a7825f3a6e9ccf63d4.png)\n\nBy rotating this curve $2\\pi$ radians about the $x$-axis, we get a **surface of revolution**:\n\n![](images/88/88c9c1ec629ab9967cc011aaadafebea255cd50abee9c3730bed4f53f671c71b.png)\n\nWe can parametrize this surface in the following way:\n\n- We take a point $P$ on the surface with $x$-coordinate equal to $x_0$ for some fixed $x_0 \\in [0,2].$\n\n- Now, by taking the intersection of the surface with the plane $x=x_0,$ we get a circle of radius $e^{-x_0}.$\n\n![](images/f1/f1e2cf1c571a21e06f1b25f99a64b5529fea8c65c897b3f225cfd0d307d725d7.png)\n\n \nThis circle can be parameterized as follows:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf r(x_0, \\theta) = \\big\\langle {\\color{black}x_0}, \\: e^{-x_0} \\, \\cos{\\theta}, \\: e^{-x_0} \\, \\sin{\\theta} \\big\\rangle,\n\\quad  \\theta \\in [0,2\\pi).\n$$\n\n- Finally, by allowing $x_0$ to vary over the interval $[0, 2],$ these circles will trace out the surface of revolution. Therefore, our parameterization is\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}(x,\\theta) = \\big\\langle x, \\: e^{-x} \\cos{\\theta}, \\: e^{-x}\\sin{\\theta} \\big\\rangle,\n\\quad \nx \\in [0,2], \n\\quad\n\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi).\n$$\n\nLet's now describe some general formulas for parametrizing surfaces of revolution." :tutorial/id #uuid "9bd13796-1bf7-5a1b-a9f4-87567fd6998c" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5305 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "1d490ab1-06f7-5e16-b643-b1b0d8fab4fe" :step/math-academy-id 33155 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186173086}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172934 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172055 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186171861 :question/id #uuid "1d32df15-74ee-5a3d-bc33-3d6452700e84" :question/math-academy-id "e-8879"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5ac4af78-af9b-5d48-a5d7-7d47ad92e348" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048259} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050722}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172216} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172217} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235460} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186247033} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186247035} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186247036} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186247037} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275400}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Parametrization of a Surface of Revolution: Rotation About the x-Axis"} :step/id #uuid "9771f588-30ca-519b-9cd3-5da25e6f18e1" :step/math-academy-id 33156 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172965}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172935 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172056 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186171862 :question/id #uuid "6ab25451-ad5c-5437-b4ba-046b0bd30fe6" :question/math-academy-id "e-8880"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b85acdfc-0505-5472-b5f3-53270bffa030" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172218} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172257} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186247043} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186247044} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186247045} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278353} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278357} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186401083} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186415536}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Parametrization of a Surface of Revolution: Rotation About the y-Axis or z-Axis"} :step/id #uuid "8409e7cf-c975-5a30-a407-4fc56e97763f" :step/math-academy-id 33157 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186173083}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172965 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186173186 :tutorial/content "Consider the point $(x(y),y,0)$ that lies on the curve $x=x(y)\\geq 0$ for $y\\in [a,b]$ in the $xy$-plane. Rotating this point by $2\\pi$ radians about the $y$-axis generates a circle, as shown below.\n\n![](images/98/981ed341198c54e5dffeae770d13b3d46222ad429d0315ecd057b43995b4c0d6.png)\n\nThe pair $(\\textrm{circle}_1,\\textrm{circle}_2)$ denotes any parametrization of a circle of radius $x(y)$ with parameter $y.$\n\nUsing arguments similar to those seen earlier, the corresponding surface of revolution can be parametrized as\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r} = \\big\\langle x(y) \\cos\\theta, \\: y, \\: x(y) \\sin\\theta \\big\\rangle, \\quad y\\in[a,b], \\quad \\theta\\in [0,2\\pi).\n$$\n\n**Note:** Since a parametrization of a circle is not unique, we can obtain different parameterizations of our surface of revolution. For example, the following is also a valid parameterization:\n\\[ \n\\mathbf{r} = \\big\\langle x(y) \\sin\\theta, \\: y, \\: x(y) \\cos\\theta \\big\\rangle, \\quad y\\in[a,b], \\quad \\theta\\in [0,2\\pi). \n\\]\n\nSimilarly, the rotation of a point $(0,y,z(y))$ on the curve $z=z(y)\\geq 0$ in the $yz$-plane about the $y$-axis can be visualized as follows:\n\n![](images/59/5936d54e55c151ec2d9786a3ce2bd807970a71c10a21974562fd601bf50dfd37.png)\n\nThe corresponding surface of revolution can be parametrized as\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r} = \\big\\langle z(y) \\cos\\theta, \\: y, \\: z(y) \\sin\\theta \\big\\rangle, \\quad y\\in[a,b], \\quad \\theta\\in [0,2\\pi).\n$$\n\nAs mentioned previously, neither of these parameterizations is unique.\n\n**Note:** Since a parametrization of a circle is not unique, we can obtain different parameterizations of our surface of revolution. For example, the following is also a valid parameterization:\n\\[\n\\mathbf{r} = \\big\\langle z(y) \\sin\\theta, \\: y, \\: z(y) \\cos\\theta \\big\\rangle, \\quad y\\in[a,b], \\quad \\theta\\in [0,2\\pi). \n\\]" :tutorial/id #uuid "ae882ec8-1ebd-5277-8224-b440b433c8ee" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5507 :tutorial/title "Formulas for Rotation About the y-Axis"} :step/id #uuid "23271cc4-5b18-5ab7-87a0-420c4bf3d6fc" :step/math-academy-id 34312 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186173087}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186173083 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172132 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186171712 :question/id #uuid "8ed57b1f-0082-5281-b061-ff2020ed7d66" :question/math-academy-id "e-12364"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4df896f9-7094-5d49-a5cf-3d0f62164298" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048384}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172233} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172379} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278362} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278367} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278368} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278372} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278373} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186401212} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186415535}] :knowledge-point/title "The Fundamental Vector Product of a Surface of Revolution"} :step/id #uuid "2109942c-c8ff-56d5-8311-ec29a24f7390" :step/math-academy-id 44310 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186173084}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186173084 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186173253 :tutorial/content "Recall that if $S$ is a surface parametrized by a differentiable vector-valued function\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}(u,v) = \\big\\langle x(u,v), \\: y(u,v), \\: z(u,v) \\big\\rangle\n$$\n\ndefined over a region $D$ in the $uv$-plane, then the surface area of $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_{S} \\: \\textrm{d}S =  \\iint\\limits_{D} \\| \\mathbf{r}'_ u \\times \\mathbf{r}'_v  \\| \\: \\textrm{d}A,\n$$\n\nwhere $\\mathbf{r}'_ u \\times \\mathbf{r}'_v$ is the fundamental vector product of the surface.\n\nLet's apply this formula to find the surface area generated by a surface of revolution.\n\nSuppose that the curve [math]y=f(x)[/math] for [math]x \\in [a,b][/math] is rotated [math]2\\pi[/math] radians about the [math]x[/math]-axis. The corresponding surface of revolution can be parametrized as\n\n\\begin{align*}\n\\mathbf{r} = \\big\\langle x, \\: f(x) \\cos\\theta, \\: f(x) \\sin\\theta \\big\\rangle, \\quad (x,\\theta) \\in D, \n\\end{align*}\n\nwhere [math]D= \\big\\{ (x,\\theta) \\: : \\: x \\in[a,b], \\: \\theta \\in [0,2\\pi) \\big\\}.[/math] How can we find the area of this surface?\n\nFirst, we now compute the tangent vectors to the grid curves:\n\n\\begin{align*}\n\\mathbf{r}'_x \n&= \\bigg\\langle \\dfrac{\\partial x}{\\partial x}, \\: \\dfrac{\\partial y}{\\partial x}, \\: \\dfrac{\\partial z}{\\partial x} \\bigg\\rangle \n= \\big\\langle 1, \\:f'(x) \\cos\\theta, \\: f'(x) \\sin\\theta \\big\\rangle \\\\[10pt]\n\n\\mathbf{r}'_\\theta \n&= \\bigg\\langle \\dfrac{\\partial x}{\\partial \\theta}, \\: \\dfrac{\\partial y}{\\partial \\theta}, \\: \\dfrac{\\partial z}{\\partial \\theta} \\bigg\\rangle \n= \\big\\langle 0, \\: -f(x) \\sin\\theta, \\: f(x) \\cos\\theta \\big\\rangle\n\\end{align*}\n\nNext, the fundamental vector product is\n\n\\begin{align*}\n\\mathbf{r}'_x \\times \\mathbf{r}'_\\theta \n&= \n\\begin{vmatrix}\n\\mathbf{i} & \\mathbf{j} & \\mathbf{k} \\\\\n1 & f'(x) \\cos\\theta & f'(x) \\sin\\theta \\\\\n0 & -f(x) \\sin\\theta & f(x) \\cos\\theta\n\\end{vmatrix} \\\\[5pt]\n&= \\bigg\\langle \\begin{vmatrix} f'(x) \\cos\\theta & f'(x) \\sin\\theta \\\\ -f(x) \\sin\\theta & f(x) \\cos\\theta \\end{vmatrix}, \\: -\\begin{vmatrix} 1 & f'(x) \\sin\\theta \\\\ 0  & f(x) \\cos\\theta \\end{vmatrix}, \\: \\begin{vmatrix} 1 & f'(x) \\cos\\theta \\\\ 0 & -f(x) \\sin\\theta  \\end{vmatrix} \\bigg\\rangle \\\\[5pt]\n&= \\big\\langle f(x)f'(x)\\cos^2\\theta+f(x)f'(x)\\sin^2\\theta, \\: -f(x) \\cos\\theta, \\: -f(x)\\sin\\theta \\big\\rangle \\\\[5pt]\n&= \\big\\langle f(x)f'(x), \\: -f(x) \\cos\\theta, \\:- f(x)\\sin\\theta \\big\\rangle\n\\end{align*}\n\nand\n\n\\begin{align*}\n\\left\\|  \\mathbf{r}'_y \\times \\mathbf{r}'_\\theta \\right\\|\n&= \\sqrt{\\big( f(x)f'(x) \\big)^2 + \\big( -f(x) \\cos\\theta \\big)^2 + \\big( -f(x)\\sin\\theta \\big)^2 } \\\\[3pt]\n&= \\sqrt{\\big(f(x)\\big)^2\\big(f'(x)\\big)^2 + \\big(f(x)\\big)^2 \\big( \\cos^2\\theta + \\sin^2\\theta \\big) } \\\\[3pt]\n&= \\sqrt{\\big(f(x)\\big)^2\\big(f'(x)\\big)^2 + \\big(f(x)\\big)^2 } \\\\[3pt]\n&= f(x) \\sqrt{1 + \\big(f'(x)\\big)^2 }.\n\\end{align*}\n\nTherefore, the surface area is\n\n\\begin{align*}\nA \n&= \\iint\\limits_S \\,  \\text{d}S \\\\[5pt]\n&= \\iint\\limits_D  \\left\\|  \\mathbf{r}'_y \\times \\mathbf{r}'_\\theta \\right\\|  \\text{d}x\\text{d}\\theta \\\\[5pt]\n&= \\int_{a}^{b} \\int_{0}^{2\\pi} f(x) \\sqrt{1 + \\big(f'(x)\\big)^2 } \\: \\text{d}\\theta \\: \\text{d}x \\\\[5pt]\n&= \\int_{a}^{b} f(x) \\sqrt{1 + \\big(f'(x)\\big)^2} \\: \\text{d}x \\cdot \\int_{0}^{2\\pi}  \\text{d}\\theta \\\\[5pt]\n&= \\int_{a}^{b} f(x) \\sqrt{1 + \\big(f'(x)\\big)^2} \\: \\text{d}x \\cdot \\big[ \\theta \\big]_{0}^{2\\pi}  \\\\[5pt]\n&= 2\\pi \\int_{a}^{b} f(x)\\sqrt{1 + \\big(f'(x)\\big)^2 } \\: \\text{d}x.\n\\end{align*}" :tutorial/id #uuid "79f42de8-4cc4-576a-b367-c08bd87f8bcd" :tutorial/math-academy-id 7638 :tutorial/title "Computing a Surface Area of Revolution"} :step/id #uuid "8c37f4ed-7df7-5a6c-855e-263d81b3f5b0" :step/math-academy-id 44313 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186173085}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186173085 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172133 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186171713 :question/id #uuid "a2ff0c76-0d26-51a2-a2fb-498ff5f85559" :question/math-academy-id "e-12365"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4d7d1683-4735-5c93-9ad1-542e987aed1d" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172232} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172380} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278375} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278376} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278377} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278378} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186371941} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186371948} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186389126} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186415537}] :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Surface of Revolution"} :step/id #uuid "3533d6b6-1b8e-5af8-b6c0-195bca89c86d" :step/math-academy-id 44314} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186173086 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186173254 :tutorial/content "Consider the point $(x,y(x),0)$ that lies on the curve $y=y(x)$ in the $xy$-plane. Rotating this point by $2\\pi$ radians about the $x$-axis generates a circle, as shown below.\n\n![](images/86/86cbc06bd3730430665744216a702ed29cc75acae33a569e00636ee53149065d.png)\n\nIn the diagram above, the pair $(\\textrm{circle}_1,\\textrm{circle}_2)$ denotes any parametrization of a circle of radius $y(x)$ with parameter $x.$\n\nIn general, given a curve $y=y(x)\\geq 0$ for $x \\in [a,b]$ that is rotated $2\\pi$ radians about the $x$-axis, the corresponding surface of revolution can be parametrized as\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r} = \\big\\langle x, \\: y(x) \\cos\\theta, \\: y(x) \\sin\\theta \\big\\rangle, \\quad x\\in[a,b], \\quad \\theta\\in [0,2\\pi).\n$$\n\nSince a parametrization of a circle is not unique, we can obtain different parameterizations of our surface of revolution. For example, the following is also a valid parameterization:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r} = \\big\\langle x, \\: y(x) \\sin\\theta, \\: y(x) \\cos\\theta \\big\\rangle, \\quad x\\in[a,b], \\quad \\theta\\in [0,2\\pi)\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "3148fb53-9ae7-5747-aa63-b79dd1bc224f" :tutorial/math-academy-id 7648 :tutorial/title "Parametrizing a Surface Generated by Rotating y=y(x) About the x-Axis"} :step/id #uuid "93cfa70b-b133-5659-9bce-ab370759c49e" :step/math-academy-id 44408 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186173089}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186173087 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186173255 :tutorial/content "Now consider the point $(x(z),0,z)$ on the curve $x=x(z)\\geq 0$ for $z\\in [a,b]$ in the $xz$-plane. Rotating this point by $2\\pi$ radians about the $z$-axis generates a circle, as shown below.\n\n![](images/50/50473a5407143c7ec49ce8c192c256bbc184703010209767507e4b922e9f4c7b.png)\n\nThe corresponding surface of revolution can be parametrized as\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r} = \\big\\langle x(z) \\cos\\theta, \\: x(z) \\sin\\theta, \\: z \\big\\rangle, \\quad z\\in[a,b], \\quad \\theta\\in [0,2\\pi).\n$$\n\nFinally, the rotation of a point $(0,y(z),z)$ on a curve $y=y(z)\\geq 0$ in the $yz$-plane about the $z$-axis can be visualized as follows:\n\n![](images/5f/5ff1574db4d105e7d69cc12ed609898d626b4977902f98dbcbcac6633ad56d2b.png)\n\nThe corresponding surface of revolution can be parametrized as\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r} = \\big\\langle y(z) \\cos\\theta, \\: y(z) \\sin\\theta, \\: z \\big\\rangle, \\quad z\\in[a,b], \\quad \\theta\\in [0,2\\pi).\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "9e8a0947-f30a-5da6-9c3b-7b93bdba56bf" :tutorial/math-academy-id 7649 :tutorial/title "Formulas for Rotation About the z-Axis"} :step/id #uuid "6100252f-8102-5700-8f5a-11a2b77335a0" :step/math-academy-id 44409 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172935}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186173089 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186173257 :tutorial/content "Now consider the point $(x,0,z(x))$ that lies on a curve $z=z(x)$ in the $xz$-plane. Rotating this point by $2\\pi$ radians about the $x$-axis generates a circle, as shown below.\n\n![](images/74/74686e9e17ce056fea730b47bdf34ee582cc83920200f0345f75b90a6f8c9833.png)\n\nIn general, given a curve $z=z(x)\\geq 0$ for $x \\in [a,b]$ that is rotated $2\\pi$ radians about the $x$-axis, the corresponding surface of revolution can be parametrized as\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r} = \\big\\langle x, \\: z(x) \\cos\\theta, \\: z(x) \\sin\\theta \\big\\rangle, \\quad z\\in[a,b], \\quad \\theta\\in [0,2\\pi).\n$$\n\nAgain, this parametrization is not unique. An alternative parametrization is\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r} = \\big\\langle x, \\: z(x) \\sin\\theta, \\: z(x) \\cos\\theta \\big\\rangle, \\quad z\\in[a,b], \\quad \\theta\\in [0,2\\pi).\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "c06437e5-d847-5d03-aac0-497649901444" :tutorial/math-academy-id 7714 :tutorial/title "Parametrizing a Surface Generated by Rotating z=z(x) About the x-Axis"} :step/id #uuid "3100ec93-73c0-5fba-bebc-ee40c07ece7d" :step/math-academy-id 44739 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172934}}] :activity/title "Surfaces of Revolution" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186172185}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046660} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186415539} :activity/id #uuid "b6ecbe9a-3094-59f8-872f-0af781e55451" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049628 :topic/id #uuid "06a51304-5013-5e6f-ab88-267fa31fcd9b" :topic/math-academy-id 3372} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186415538 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186415536 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0244fab1-dcd6-5c87-8769-552570471608" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\sqrt{{z}^{2}-1}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415511} {:answer/id #uuid "5ceeb26c-796b-593b-80a8-390a78233aa1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\sqrt{1-{z}^{2}}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415518} {:answer/id #uuid "5f50a75f-3d11-54f7-b72f-ea3bd8cf01f5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\sqrt{1-{z}^{2}}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415519} {:answer/id #uuid "645d906f-848f-5e5e-92cb-479ed4f50490" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\sqrt{1-{z}^{2}}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415521} {:answer/id #uuid "aa5125a1-4c8d-52e7-9aac-67e23cb420b7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\sqrt{{z}^{2}-1}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415526}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186415511} :answer-field/id #uuid "638d8ce0-aca0-5ff6-85a7-9e3d7a370c18" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415533}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "a20cac79-e438-5720-941d-255bfdc76ec3" :question/math-academy-id "q-161037" :question/problem "$$\nr(z,\\theta )=⟨\\sqrt{{z}^{2}-1}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\text{__________},\\,z⟩\n$$\n\nThe vector-valued function $r(z,\\theta )$ above parametrizes the surface of revolution generated by rotating the curve $x=\\sqrt{{z}^{2}-1}$ by  $2\\pi $ radians about the $z$-axis for $z\\in [1,5].$ What is the second  component of $r?$" :question/worked-solution "![](images/d3/d3302b5f4ea4c0387d70ac8f78e03e486fff9579f1a6b5e5cec08a31cde76af3.png)\n\n \n\nA point $(x,y,z)$ on the surface generated by rotating the graph of $x=f(z)$ by $2\\pi $ radians about the $z$-axis has the position vector\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r=⟨f(z)\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,f(z)\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,z⟩,\\,z\\in [a,b],\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, notice that $x=f(z)=\\sqrt{{z}^{2}-1}\\ge 0$ for all $z\\in [1,5].$   \n\nSo, the parametrization of our surface of revolution is\n\n$$\nr(z,\\theta )=⟨\\sqrt{{z}^{2}-1}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\sqrt{{z}^{2}-1}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,z⟩,\\,z\\in [1,5],\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nTherefore, the second component of $r$ is $\\sqrt{{z}^{2}-1}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .$"} :step/id #uuid "00cc2357-080e-51b5-a1e7-01af77a068b5" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186415542}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186415539 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172233 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "74de9016-aec8-5c6f-9c78-665b929a97ce" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{sin}^{2}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415522} {:answer/id #uuid "a106d988-56e8-55fb-9494-97df8b832cbd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sin}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415525} {:answer/id #uuid "c191f424-9120-5984-b367-462661abdf0f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415527} {:answer/id #uuid "dcf41d2a-2802-588f-84c7-9bab46e26c74" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{cos}^{2}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415529} {:answer/id #uuid "e38d5fae-3d16-5915-8cda-f00c09b669d1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cos}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415530}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186415527} :answer-field/id #uuid "247cda82-3b8b-57b4-bd85-1eaf3e646212" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415531}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "ff19c12b-ba54-5c73-b456-a37b42a5dcc1" :question/math-academy-id "q-106277" :question/problem "$$\n⟨\\text{_______}\\,,\\,-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ⟩\n$$\n\nThe fundamental vector product of the surface of revolution generated by rotating the curve $y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x$ by $2\\pi $ radians about the $x$-axis for $x\\in [0,\\pi ]$ is given above. What is the $1$st component of the product?" :question/worked-solution "Notice that $y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\ge 0$ for all $x\\in [0,\\pi ].$ The parametrization of our surface of revolution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r=⟨x,f(x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,f(x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ⟩,\\,(x,\\theta )\\in D\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $f(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x,$ and\n\n$$\nD={(x,\\theta )\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le \\pi ,\\,0\\le \\theta <2\\pi }.\n$$\n\nWe now compute the tangent vectors to the grid curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{x}^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂x},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂x},\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂x}⟩=⟨1,\\,{f}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,{f}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ⟩ \\\\ {r}_{\\theta }^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂\\theta },\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂\\theta },\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂\\theta }⟩=⟨0,\\,-f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,f\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{x}^{′}\\times {r}_{\\theta }^{′} & =|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & {f}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & {f}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ 0 & -f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & f\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =⟨|\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & {f}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ -f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & f\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}|,\\,-|\\begin{aligned}1 & {f}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ 0 & f\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}|,\\,|\\begin{aligned}1 & {f}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ 0 & -f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}|⟩ \\\\  & =⟨f{f}^{′}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +f{f}^{′}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta ,\\,-f\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,-f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ⟩ \\\\  & =⟨f{f}^{′},\\,-f\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,-f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $f=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x,$ we obtain ${f}^{′}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.$ So, finally, we conclude that the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{x}^{′}\\times {r}_{\\theta }^{′}=⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x,\\,-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "62191b79-d61b-528b-ad18-dd19267394a2" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186415538}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186415540 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186371941 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "19fd857a-69bb-52bc-8fb2-b57c6fb65bf8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\pi {\\int }_{-1}^{1}(1+{z}^{4})\\sqrt{1+8{z}^{6}}\\,\\text{d}z" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371943} {:answer/id #uuid "1146da46-595d-55c2-874f-d7177d74a0a6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\pi {\\int }_{-1}^{1}(1+{z}^{4})\\sqrt{1+8{z}^{6}}\\,\\text{d}z" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371944} {:answer/id #uuid "fc506864-2f86-51e9-b176-0329e981f791" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\pi {\\int }_{-1}^{1}(1+{z}^{4})\\sqrt{1+16{z}^{8}}\\,\\text{d}z" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371945} {:answer/id #uuid "82ddc8e9-2ce1-5932-875c-df7ef67f9b63" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\pi {\\int }_{-1}^{1}(1+{z}^{4})\\sqrt{1+16{z}^{6}}\\,\\text{d}z" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371946} {:answer/id #uuid "67776efe-de42-57c3-8ff4-ba76605d7146" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\pi {\\int }_{-1}^{1}(1+{z}^{4})\\sqrt{1+16{z}^{6}}\\,\\text{d}z" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371947}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186371947} :answer-field/id #uuid "83cff6c6-9176-54ce-9248-48c8a44d7c5a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371942}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "b5f49f4b-9d27-5249-8593-5beec661bda6" :question/math-academy-id "q-161096" :question/problem "Use a fundamental vector product to determine which of the following integrals gives the surface area generated when the curve $x={z}^{4}+1$ is rotated by $2\\pi $ radians about the $z$-axis for $z\\in [-1,1].$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that $x={z}^{4}+1\\ge 0$ for all $z\\in [-1,1].$ The parametrization of our surface of revolution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r=⟨f(z)\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,f(z)\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,z⟩,\\,(z,\\theta )\\in D\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $f(z)=1+{z}^{4},$ and\n\n$$\nD={(z,\\theta )\\,:\\,-1\\le z\\le 1,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le 2\\pi }.\n$$\n\nWe now compute the tangent vectors to the grid curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{z}^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂z},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂z},\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂z}⟩=⟨{f}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,{f}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,1⟩ \\\\ {r}_{\\theta }^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂\\theta },\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂\\theta },\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂\\theta }⟩=⟨-f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,f(y)\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{z}^{′}\\times {r}_{\\theta }^{′} & =|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ {f}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & {f}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & 1 \\\\ -f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & f\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & 0\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =⟨|\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & 1 \\\\ f\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & 0\\end{aligned}|,\\,-|\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & 1 \\\\ -f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & 0\\end{aligned}|,\\,|\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & {f}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ -f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & f\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}|⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-f\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,-f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,{f}^{′}f{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{f}^{′}f{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta ⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-f\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,-f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,{f}^{′}f⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nand\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖{r}_{z}^{′}\\times {r}_{\\theta }^{′}‖ & =\\sqrt{(-f\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}+(-f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2}+({f}^{′}f)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{f}^{2}({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )+{f}^{2}({f}^{′})^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{f}^{2}+{f}^{2}({f}^{′})^{2}} \\\\  & =f\\sqrt{1+({f}^{′})^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface area is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ={∬}_{S}\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}‖{r}_{z}^{′}\\times {r}_{\\theta }^{′}‖\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}f\\sqrt{1+({f}^{′})^{2}}\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}f\\sqrt{1+({f}^{′})^{2}}{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\,\\text{d}\\theta \\,\\text{d}z \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}f\\sqrt{1+({f}^{′})^{2}}\\cdot \\theta \\,{|}_{0}^{2\\pi }\\,\\text{d}z \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{-1}^{1}f\\sqrt{1+({f}^{′})^{2}}\\,\\text{d}z.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $f(z)=1+{z}^{4},$ we obtain ${f}^{′}(z)=4{z}^{3}.$ So, finally, we conclude that our expression for the surface area is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\pi {\\int }_{-1}^{1}f\\sqrt{1+({f}^{′})^{2}}\\,\\text{d}z & =2\\pi {\\int }_{-1}^{1}(1+{z}^{4})\\sqrt{1+(4{z}^{3})^{2}}\\,\\text{d}z \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{-1}^{1}(1+{z}^{4})\\sqrt{1+16{z}^{6}}\\,\\text{d}z.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "883b0834-7022-5d35-a3fd-a77950a73c82"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186415541 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186415537 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "066318d7-b3c3-582b-8704-baaa946c3602" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\pi {\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{y}{2})\\sqrt{1+{sin}^{2}⁡(\\frac{y}{2})}\\,\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415512} {:answer/id #uuid "1a49bca9-5ff7-58a4-97d5-3b205c07eee8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\pi {\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{y}{2})\\sqrt{1+\\frac{1}{4}{sin}^{2}⁡(\\frac{y}{2})}\\,\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415514} {:answer/id #uuid "4ddf6f00-c938-50e9-9408-30046a58b1f1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\pi {\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{y}{2})\\sqrt{1+\\frac{1}{4}{sin}^{2}⁡(\\frac{y}{2})}\\,\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415516} {:answer/id #uuid "6426d8d3-9d04-5e0f-a517-240093a9074a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\pi {\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{y}{2})\\sqrt{1+{cos}^{2}⁡(\\frac{y}{2})}\\,\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415520} {:answer/id #uuid "8a136f45-dabb-5965-b74e-f1e60e2efbab" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\pi {\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{y}{2})\\sqrt{1+{sin}^{2}⁡(\\frac{y}{2})}\\,\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415524}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186415516} :answer-field/id #uuid "47b1c789-64a3-55f5-9e7d-ac42dc183f0e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415532}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "b858f50b-1a14-576c-973e-c7199e8e8cdb" :question/math-academy-id "q-161090" :question/problem "Use a fundamental vector product to determine which of the following integrals gives the surface area generated when the curve $x=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{y}{2})$ is rotated by $2\\pi $ radians about the $y$-axis for $y\\in [-\\pi ,\\pi ].$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that $x=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{y}{2})\\ge 0$ for all $y\\in [-\\pi ,\\pi ].$ The parametrization of our surface of revolution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r=⟨f(y)\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y,\\,f(y)\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ⟩,\\,(y,\\theta )\\in D\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $f(y)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{y}{2}),$ and\n\n$$\nD={(y,\\theta )\\,:\\,-\\pi \\le y\\le \\pi ,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le 2\\pi }.\n$$\n\nWe now compute the tangent vectors to the grid curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{y}^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂y},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂y}\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂y}⟩=⟨{f}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,1,\\,{f}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ⟩ \\\\ {r}_{\\theta }^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂\\theta },\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂\\theta },\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂\\theta }⟩=⟨-f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,0,\\,f\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{y}^{′}\\times {r}_{\\theta }^{′} & =|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ {f}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & 1 & {f}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ -f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & 0 & f\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =⟨|\\begin{aligned}1 & {f}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ 0 & f\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}|,\\,-|\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & {f}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ -f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & f\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}|,\\,|\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & 1 \\\\ -f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & 0\\end{aligned}|⟩ \\\\  & =⟨f\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,-f{f}^{′}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta -f{f}^{′}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta ,\\,f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ⟩ \\\\  & =⟨f\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,-f{f}^{′},\\,f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖{r}_{y}^{′}\\times {r}_{\\theta }^{′}‖ & =\\sqrt{(f\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}+(-f{f}^{′})^{2}+(f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{f}^{2}({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )+{f}^{2}({f}^{′})^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{f}^{2}+{f}^{2}({f}^{′})^{2}} \\\\  & =f\\sqrt{1+({f}^{′})^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface area is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ={∬}_{S}\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}‖{r}_{y}^{′}\\times {r}_{\\theta }^{′}‖\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}f\\sqrt{1+({f}^{′})^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }f\\sqrt{1+({f}^{′})^{2}}{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\,\\text{d}\\theta \\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }f\\sqrt{1+({f}^{′})^{2}}\\cdot \\theta \\,{|}_{0}^{2\\pi }\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }f\\sqrt{1+({f}^{′})^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $f(y)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{y}{2}),$ we obtain ${f}^{′}(y)=-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{y}{2}).$ So, finally, we conclude that our expression for the surface area is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\pi {\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }f\\sqrt{1+({f}^{′})^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y & =2\\pi {\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{y}{2})\\sqrt{1+{(-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{y}{2}))}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{y}{2})\\sqrt{1+\\frac{1}{4}{sin}^{2}⁡(\\frac{y}{2})}\\,\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "8f3fca3a-1b91-5ef9-86dc-c2de3c3b545f" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186415540}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186415542 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186415535 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0a05c2e1-fb8c-50d3-b809-4010efe33716" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{2}{(z-1)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415513} {:answer/id #uuid "41b7e536-caa1-5770-93ef-a558a35bf097" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{(z-1)}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415515} {:answer/id #uuid "5238fd8c-d50c-56c0-a63c-162bd3f52406" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{z-1}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415517} {:answer/id #uuid "79004dcb-4e5d-5f5d-8fa0-3bf03f93e5ca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415523} {:answer/id #uuid "da7f796f-a386-5f76-a134-0786b69fca45" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{z-1}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415528}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186415523} :answer-field/id #uuid "f2a4e677-4714-5cf2-98da-8e4b0964eb3f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415534}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "0c98998f-cfca-568f-8a23-cab1aa107bdb" :question/math-academy-id "q-161060" :question/problem "$$\n⟨\\,\\sqrt{z-1}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,,\\,\\sqrt{z-1}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\,,\\,\\text{_______}⟩\n$$\n\n \n\nThe fundamental vector product of the surface of revolution generated by rotating the curve $y=\\sqrt{z-1}$ by $2\\pi $ radians about the $z$-axis for $z\\in [1,10]$ is given above. What is the $3$rd component of the product?" :question/worked-solution "Notice that $y=\\sqrt{z-1}\\ge 0$ for all $z\\in [1,10].$ The parametrization of our surface of revolution is  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r=⟨f(z)\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,f(z)\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,z⟩,\\,(z,\\theta )\\in D\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $f(z)=\\sqrt{z-1},$ and  \n\n$$\nD={(z,\\theta )\\,:\\,1\\le z\\le 10,\\,0\\le \\theta <2\\pi }.\n$$\n\nWe now compute the tangent vectors to the grid curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{z}^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂z},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂z},\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂z}⟩=⟨{f}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,{f}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,1⟩ \\\\ {r}_{\\theta }^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂\\theta },\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂\\theta },\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂\\theta }⟩=⟨f\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,-f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{z}^{′}\\times {r}_{\\theta }^{′} & =|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ {f}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & {f}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & 1 \\\\ f\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & -f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & 0\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =⟨|\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & 1 \\\\ -f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & 0\\end{aligned}|,\\,-|\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & 1 \\\\ f\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & 0\\end{aligned}|,\\,|\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & {f}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ f\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & -f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}|⟩ \\\\  & =⟨f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,f\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,-{f}^{′}f{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta -{f}^{′}f{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta ⟩ \\\\  & =⟨f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,f\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,-{f}^{′}f⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $f=\\sqrt{z-1},$ we obtain ${f}^{′}=\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{z-1}}.$ So, finally, we conclude that the fundamental vector product is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{z}^{′}\\times {r}_{\\theta }^{′}=⟨\\sqrt{z-1}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\sqrt{z-1}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,-\\frac{1}{2}⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "d593c770-7b73-5f58-8766-9937826ee041" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186415541}}] :activity/title "Surfaces of Revolution" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415510}] [{:activity/expected-seconds 600.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130288} :activity/id #uuid "cf0445f2-a611-590c-9874-d90229e6ea42" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050098 :topic/id #uuid "2d131eff-4143-5f33-ab1b-d2795c01303f" :topic/math-academy-id 2088} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130288 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130360 :tutorial/content "An **initial value problem (IVP)** for a homogeneous linear system has the form\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}'(t)=A\\mathbf{x}(t),\n\\qquad \n\\mathbf{x}(t_0)=\\mathbf{x}_0,\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is a constant $2 \\times 2$ matrix and $\\mathbf{x}_0\\in\\mathbb{R}^2$ is the given initial state at $t=t_0.$ In other words, in an IVP we seek the solution of $\\mathbf{x}'(t)=A\\mathbf{x}(t)$ that passes through the point $\\mathbf{x}_0$ at $t=t_0.$\n\nTo solve an IVP, we proceed as follows:\n\n- Find the general solution of the system $\\mathbf{x}'(t)=A\\mathbf{x}(t){:}$ \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}(t)=c_1\\mathbf{x}_1(t)+c_2\\mathbf{x}_2(t)\n$$\n\n- Substitute $t=t_0$ into the general solution and use the initial condition $\\mathbf{x}(t_0)=\\mathbf{x}_0{:}$\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}(t_0)=c_1\\mathbf{x}_1(t_0)+c_2\\mathbf{x}_2(t_0)\n\\qquad\\Rightarrow\\qquad\nc_1\\mathbf{x}_1(t_0)+c_2\\mathbf{x}_2(t_0)=\\mathbf{x}_0\n$$\n\n- Solve the resulting linear system for $c_1$ and $c_2,$ then substitute those values back into $\\mathbf{x}(t).$\n\nThe definition (and the procedure) above generalizes directly to the $n\\times n$ case.\n\nLet's see some concrete examples of solving an IVP." :tutorial/id #uuid "92ec67c9-079c-5825-825b-08cca4b2cf25" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3651 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "c26cbdb9-e4ae-57e4-8ad0-478213ccd227" :step/math-academy-id 587180 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130289}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130289 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129311 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186128884 :question/id #uuid "e16376f2-ffb9-580c-a7e7-57f5814f8289" :question/math-academy-id "e-21137"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "83399378-8815-5602-a60a-6901114416f7" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049852}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129578} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129584} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302280} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302281} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302282} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302283} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186370049} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186413788} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186413789}] :knowledge-point/title "Solving an Initial Value Problem Given the General Solution"} :step/id #uuid "d0ac3561-63c3-5604-a2d7-f38c8ca2d824" :step/math-academy-id 587181 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130290}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130290 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129312 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186128954 :question/id #uuid "226a5f8f-ac26-5f3e-8f8f-f145c314820f" :question/math-academy-id "e-5609"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "35a8694d-4fa1-54a6-a492-3025cb627fa1" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048452}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129725} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129726} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186326756} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186371105} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186389608} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186389615} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186393961} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186413787} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186413791}] :knowledge-point/title "Solving an Initial Value Problem Given the Eigenvectors and Eigenvalues of the Corresponding Matrix"} :step/id #uuid "89c5622e-b252-5578-bb6d-ccdb5821b61d" :step/math-academy-id 587184 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130291}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130291 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129313 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186128953 :question/id #uuid "d2768264-6d1a-5e62-9af4-025c877e138b" :question/math-academy-id "e-5608"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "833d0ec0-d895-5d66-b2ad-d058858caf9e" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048775} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050001}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129582} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129583} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302319} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302324} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186370065} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186390777} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186390784} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186413790}] :knowledge-point/title "Solving an Initial Value Problem for a Linear System of Differential Equations"} :step/id #uuid "0fe5a7b9-5aff-56d9-8bcf-d38aed54ee82" :step/math-academy-id 587187}] :activity/title "Solving Homogeneous Systems of ODEs With Distinct Eigenvalues and Initial Conditions" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186129399}] [{:activity/expected-seconds 420.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158230} :activity/id #uuid "44aff9fc-2508-51a9-baf1-3f855c1f0a4c" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050606 :topic/id #uuid "57fe5849-4b30-59b2-99bd-4fb2b776d659" :topic/math-academy-id 2980} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158230 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158531 :tutorial/content "In statistics, we often want to use sample data to deduce (or **infer**) the values of various **population parameters**. The two most common population parameters that we wish to find are\n\n- the **population mean** $\\mu,$ and\n\n- the **population variance** $\\sigma^2.$\n\nThe population mean is the mean of the *entire* population. Likewise, the population variance is the variance of the *entire* population. We should consider these values as *fixed* unknowns. \n\nSuppose, for example, we want to find the mean number of pages per book at a particular library. In other words, we wish to find the population mean $\\mu.$ However, it is impractical to count the pages in every book. So, instead, we might approximate $\\mu$ using the following sampling procedure:\n\n1\\. Randomly choose, say, $5$ books off the shelf.\n\n2\\. Count the number of pages in each book.\n\n3\\. Compute the mean of the $5$ observations.\n\nIn this case, the **sample size** $n=5.$\n\nSuppose we follow this procedure and get the following **observations**:\n\n$$\n201, \\qquad 506, \\qquad 58, \\qquad 104, \\qquad 99\n$$\n\nIt's convenient to denote the individual observations as $x_i.$ So, in this case, we have \n\n$$\nx_1 = 201,\\qquad x_2 = 506,\\qquad \\ldots,\\qquad x_5 = 99.\n$$\n\nWe can compute the mean $\\overline{x}$ of our sample as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{―}{x} & =\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}(201+506+58+104+99) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}\\cdot 968 \\\\  & =193.6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n The value $\\bar{x} = 193.6$ is called an **estimate** of the population mean $\\mu.$ We sometimes write $\\hat{\\mu} = 193.6$ to denote that the value $193.6$ is an estimate of $\\mu.$\n\nIt's important to realize that our estimate $\\bar{x}$ depends entirely on this particular sample. We'd get a different estimate for $\\mu$ if we took a different sample." :tutorial/id #uuid "359539bd-254d-5083-aa76-577b8a159438" :tutorial/math-academy-id 4738 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "fab3a24b-2709-54fe-985c-5fe95ec0712c" :step/math-academy-id 29343 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158287}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158231 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157355 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157172 :question/id #uuid "a485378b-0bce-5576-bcbc-2ef77df2da17" :question/math-academy-id "e-7882"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b60ce582-4127-57b8-bba8-04cf17169ca3" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050862}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157964} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157965} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186412752} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186412754} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186412755} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186412757} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186412760} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186433274} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186433276} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186433277} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186433278} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186433282}] :knowledge-point/title "Estimating a Population Mean From the Sample Mean"} :step/id #uuid "a21dbb27-5fb2-54ba-a76f-f7231e67b5fd" :step/math-academy-id 29347 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158288}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158287 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158560 :tutorial/content "It is helpful to think of the observations of a particular sample\n\n$$\nx_1,\\quad x_2,\\quad \\ldots,\\quad x_n\n$$\n\nas a particular instance of data generated by a sequence of random variables \n\n$$\nX_1,\\quad X_2,\\quad \\ldots,\\quad X_n.\n$$\n\nLet's make sure we're clear about the notation:\n\n- $X_i$ is a *random variable* that denotes the $i$th observation in a random sample. Its value changes when we conduct a new sample.\n\n- $x_i$ is the value of the $i$th observation in a particular sample.\n\nWe typically assume that our sequence of random variables $X_1, X_2,\\ldots, X_n$ has the following properties:\n\n- They are **independent and identically distributed** (or **I.I.D.**), meaning they are independent and have the same probability distribution. Moreover, they have the same distribution as the population.\n\n- Their mean is equal to the population mean:\n\n$$\n\\textrm{E}(X_i) = \\mu, \\qquad 1\\leq i\\leq n\n$$\n\n- Their variance is equal to the population variance:\n\n$$\n\\textrm{Var}(X_i) = \\sigma^2, \\qquad 1\\leq i\\leq n\n$$\n\nWe often refer to a sequence of random variables $X_1, X_2,\\ldots, X_n$ with these properties as a **random sample of size** $\\boldsymbol{n}.$\n\nIn general, if $X_1, X_2, \\ldots, X_{n}$ is a random sample of size $n,$ then the **sample mean** is given by\n\n$$\n\\overline{X} \n= \\dfrac1n\\sum_{i=1}^n X_i .\n$$\n\nWe say that the sample mean $\\overline{X}$ is an **estimator** of the population mean $\\mu.$\n\nSince $X_1, X_2, \\ldots, X_n$ are random variables, so is $\\overline{X}.$ The probability distribution of $\\overline{X}$ is called the **sampling distribution** of $\\overline{X}.$ The sampling distribution of $\\overline{X}$ is an important topic we'll discuss in detail in a future lesson." :tutorial/id #uuid "b79ae73d-8ebe-5dda-916d-6eeb61ce850d" :tutorial/math-academy-id 6297 :tutorial/title "The Sample Mean"} :step/id #uuid "c4fadf35-c701-59e2-9c8b-d385cd3565ae" :step/math-academy-id 38136 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158231}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158288 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158561 :tutorial/content "One important feature of $\\overline{X}$ is that it's an **unbiased estimator** of $\\mu.$ This means that the expected value of $\\overline{X}$ equals $\\mu,$ that is\n\n$$\n\\textrm{E}[\\overline{X}] = \\mu.\n$$\n\n \n\nWe can easily show that $\\overline{X}$ is an unbiased estimator of $\\mu$ using the properties of expectation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[\\overset{―}{X}] & =\\text{E}[\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{X}_{i}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{n}\\cdot \\text{E}[{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{X}_{i}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{n}\\cdot \\text{E}[{X}_{1}+{X}_{2}+⋯+{X}_{n}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{n}\\cdot (\\text{E}[{X}_{1}]+\\text{E}[{X}_{2}]+⋯+\\text{E}[{X}_{n}]) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{n}\\cdot (\\mu +\\mu +⋯+\\mu ) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{n}\\cdot n\\mu  \\\\  & =\\mu \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe fact that $\\overline{X}$ is an unbiased estimator of $\\mu$ is a desirable property. We'll meet so-called **biased estimators** in future lessons. \n\nFinally, any estimate $\\bar{x}$ of the population mean $\\mu$ found by computing the mean of a sample is called an **unbiased estimate** of $\\mu.$" :tutorial/id #uuid "4c9ca398-f024-59cf-b484-80cf2f43cc8c" :tutorial/math-academy-id 6298 :tutorial/title "The Sample Mean as an Unbiased Estimator"} :step/id #uuid "5d85b5ed-e218-547b-81cf-36a60c612b6f" :step/math-academy-id 38137 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158289}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158289 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157392 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186156993 :question/id #uuid "33bedb30-2c99-52e8-a665-e3bddeed7e13" :question/math-academy-id "e-10114"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "38455e74-50c2-5581-b9e1-a8dd0c85f826" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048317} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048508}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157598} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157599} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186412753} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186412756} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186412758} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186412759} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186433275} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186433279} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186433280} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186433281}] :knowledge-point/title "The Expected Value of the Sample Mean"} :step/id #uuid "2edb1e12-aed2-5310-a098-962a8588a4f0" :step/math-academy-id 38142}] :activity/title "The Sample Mean" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186157528}] [{:activity/expected-seconds 420.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180479} :activity/id #uuid "545f2edf-8d2f-583e-8ce1-05c1095b6170" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050687 :topic/id #uuid "5f0fbf1a-01fe-5c95-8b44-55eb40e24c08" :topic/math-academy-id 3708} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180479 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180713 :tutorial/content "The line integral with respect to $x$ of the function $f(x,y)$ along the path $C$ can be calculated using the formula\n\n$$\n\\int_C f(x,y)\\,\\textrm d x = \\int\\limits_a^b  f(\\mathbf r(t)) \\, \\dfrac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} \\, \\textrm{d}t,\n$$\n\nwhere $\\mathbf r (t)$ for $a\\leq t\\leq b$ is a parametrization of $C.$ \n\nNow, if we set $f(x,y) = 1,$ then by the change of variables formula, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{a}^{b}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{{x}_{1}}^{{x}_{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={x}_{2}-{x}_{1},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $x_1$ and $x_2$ are the $x$-coordinates at the initial and terminal points of $C,$ respectively. To summarize,\n\n$$\n\\int_C \\,\\textrm d x = x_2 - x_1.\n$$\n\nLet's use this result to calculate $\\displaystyle\\int_C\\,\\textrm d x,$ where $C$ is the path along the quarter-circle of radius $2$ centered at $O$ in the first quadrant, traversed in the clockwise direction, as shown below:\n\n![](images/01/01b5f21b2c778f62f7dd884065ee4ce17dd01bd021063fc7c788f09a89feea86.png)\n\nThe initial point is $(0,2)$ and the terminal point is $(2,0).$ So, we have $x_1 = 0, x_2 = 2,$ and therefore,\n\n$$\n\\int_C \\,\\textrm d x = 2-0 = 2.\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "c57d3ca1-3c93-5d88-8e4f-5bee849ea14e" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5814 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "e23b5a77-ead0-581f-bcc1-2f32ba803361" :step/math-academy-id 35641 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180483}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180480 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179731 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179593 :question/id #uuid "30d54dcc-49a2-5cd0-9eb9-659413ed5c67" :question/math-academy-id "e-9525"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "79e281dd-40c0-515a-a5d5-d063609ecf73" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186051000}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179999} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180000} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259650} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259665} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259669} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186415676} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186415679} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186415680} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186415681} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186415682} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186426488} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186426489}] :knowledge-point/title "Line Integrals of Unity With Respect to X"} :step/id #uuid "ba17887b-5323-555c-9d19-93a2e99bcb3d" :step/math-academy-id 35654 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180485}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180481 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179732 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179594 :question/id #uuid "d799bcde-dbf0-580e-921b-18e56d729ea1" :question/math-academy-id "e-9526"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e6935944-0acd-5593-aa80-e56f6014c13f" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179996} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180001} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259670} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259671} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259672} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259673} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259674} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259675} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259677} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259678} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259679} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259680}] :knowledge-point/title "Line Integrals of Unity With Respect to Y"} :step/id #uuid "97614692-6b8b-51e8-88ea-05390514a3cc" :step/math-academy-id 35655 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180484}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180482 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179733 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179595 :question/id #uuid "e1d24f1c-4b7c-5c5e-bf93-7f9e76dffdf2" :question/math-academy-id "e-9527"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2fd19952-77af-5f27-a7ef-adf46b1958ae" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048563}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180002} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180003} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259608} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259609} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259610} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259611} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259612} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259613} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259614} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259615} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186415677} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186415678}] :knowledge-point/title "Orientation-Dependence"} :step/id #uuid "5792d95e-415c-55aa-9d58-b81c51e1404f" :step/math-academy-id 35660 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180486}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180483 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180714 :tutorial/content "In addition, we note the following regarding line integrals with respect to $x\\mathbin{:}$\n\n- If $C = C_1\\cup C_2$ is a piecewise-smooth curve, then\n\n$$\n\\int_C f(x,y)\\,\\textrm d x = \\int_{C_1} f(x,y)\\,\\textrm d x +  \\int_{C_2} f(x,y)\\,\\textrm d x.\n$$\n\n- When $C$ is a *closed* curve, we sometimes use the following notation:\n\n$$\n\\oint_C f(x,y)\\,\\textrm d x\n$$\n\n \nIf the curve is traversed once, the initial and terminal points are the same, so $x_1 = x_2.$ Therefore, for line integrals of unity with respect to $x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\oint_C \\,\\textrm d x = 0.\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "7c1ae4ef-4256-598c-b44d-3421c8c4ebe1" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5817 :tutorial/title "Some Remarks"} :step/id #uuid "b7182be2-ddc6-5d8c-9367-0bf313338059" :step/math-academy-id 35673 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180480}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180484 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180715 :tutorial/content "Earlier, we showed that\n\n$$\n\\int_{C}\\,\\textrm d x = 2,\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is the path along the quarter-circle shown below.\n\n![](images/01/01b5f21b2c778f62f7dd884065ee4ce17dd01bd021063fc7c788f09a89feea86.png)\n\nIf we reverse the orientation of the curve, we get the following path:\n\n![](images/46/469675e85a6ca1d23de6da985d36e8e86c859ceff38ea6849984ea3c2cd34abe.png)\n\nThe initial and terminal points have swapped, and so we now have $x_1 = 2$ and $x_2 = 0.$ Applying our formula, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-C}\\,\\text{d}x & ={x}_{2}-{x}_{1} \\\\  & =0-2 \\\\  & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis example illustrates an important property of line integrals with respect to $x,$ described in full as follows:\n\n*Let $C$ be a piecewise-smooth path in the $xy$-plane, and let $-C$ be the curve consisting of the same points as $C$ but with the opposite orientation. Then,*\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle\\int_{-C} f(x,y)\\,\\textrm d x = -\\int_{C} f(x,y)\\,\\textrm d x.\n$$\n\n*Similarly, *\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle\\int_{-C} f(x,y)\\,\\textrm d y = -\\int_{C} f(x,y)\\,\\textrm d y.\n$$\n\nIn other words, line integrals with respect to $x$ and $y$ are *dependent on orientation*. This is quite different from the case for line integrals with respect to arc length, which are independent of orientation." :tutorial/id #uuid "6fb52502-a498-5599-851c-64cf19d11bf3" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5818 :tutorial/title "Further Properties of Line Integrals With Respect to X and Y"} :step/id #uuid "fb92124a-a40c-5b95-8acb-21f039c39f10" :step/math-academy-id 35674 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180482}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180485 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180716 :tutorial/content "Similar to line integrals of unity with respect to $x,$ the line integral of unity with respect to $y$ along a path $C$ is equal to the net change in $y$ between the terminal and initial points:\n\n$$\n\\int_C \\,\\textrm d y = y_2 - y_1,\n$$\n\nwhere $y_2$ is the $y$-coordinate of the terminal point, and $y_1$ is the $y$-coordinate of the initial point. \n\nAlso, if $C$ is a closed, positively oriented curve traversed once in the counterclockwise direction, then\n\n$$\n\\oint_C \\,\\textrm d y = 0.\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "16485104-9964-58c8-8611-2769190cbf9c" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5819 :tutorial/title "Line Integrals of Unity With Respect to Y"} :step/id #uuid "e5542a0f-35f9-5d2c-a937-679f3e3b6ef6" :step/math-academy-id 35675 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180481}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180486 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180717 :tutorial/content "![](images/c2/c2ab6be66d91822186ee94c846f8cd1a247da776b89d7d9220b836193b9b04d3.png)\n\nConsider the path $C$ above. Earlier, we showed that\n\n$$\n\\int_{C}\\,\\textrm d x = 2,\n$$\n\nWe note the following:\n\n- In the context of our usual interpretation of line integrals with respect to $x,$ this result represents the total area accumulated when the function $z=1$ is projected onto the plane $y=0$ as $C$ is traversed from the initial point $(0,2)$ to the terminal point $(2,0).$ \n\n![](images/29/297e4e302b0cec7f4448ec7e8b36ab6c1f84d79d5f71aad1c6887d86d54dc328.png)\n\nFrom the diagram, we see that this projection generates a rectangle of length $x_2 - x_1 = 2$ and height $z=1.$ Since the integral of unity with respect to $x$ along $C$ is equal to the area of this rectangle, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}x & =1\\cdot ({x}_{2}-{x}_{1}) \\\\  & ={x}_{2}-{x}_{1} \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFurthermore, this result is *positive* because the rectangle is traced out in the direction of *increasing* $x.$\n\n- If we reverse the direction of the curve then we get a *negative* result. This is because the rectangle is traced out in the direction of *decreasing* $x,$ as shown below.\n\n![](images/17/179713734445f85818ef4e46cfa5948115cea936538acd3095cbfa706d381972.png)" :tutorial/id #uuid "1ba2d901-3b8c-5ce9-a7bd-ace7643f3277" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5820 :tutorial/title "Intuition Behind Some of the Results"} :step/id #uuid "a896d261-9315-51e1-80bb-4191a37e68b3" :step/math-academy-id 35676}] :activity/title "Properties of Line Integrals With Respect to X and Y" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186179861}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046660} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186426490} :activity/id #uuid "76e8a030-4b20-5150-8ff6-5733d6c02963" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050687 :topic/id #uuid "5f0fbf1a-01fe-5c95-8b44-55eb40e24c08" :topic/math-academy-id 3708} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186426490 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259608 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "075ffae1-ea68-5f4d-bb63-f49b56d8e6b0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "Impossible to determine" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256845} {:answer/id #uuid "51a75927-20c3-52d1-a64d-8ec72f7cabd5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257476} {:answer/id #uuid "645886ef-4aa0-5f2b-bb93-dc06146a70f0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257662} {:answer/id #uuid "7afaaf8e-21fa-551e-b15c-0317c5d6ef18" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257872} {:answer/id #uuid "99fb9ef2-4ee0-53a2-a318-7d1eb71ac286" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258136}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186257476} :answer-field/id #uuid "cb49f773-46c7-535f-a9e1-dc1765ec0827" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259390}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "42bfa60d-7b26-5dc6-9634-a2e58886024d" :question/math-academy-id "q-124179" :question/problem "If ${\\int }_{-{C}_{1}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y=3$ and ${\\int }_{{C}_{2}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y=5,$ calculate ${\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y$ given that $C={C}_{1}\\cup {C}_{2}.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $C={C}_{1}\\cup {C}_{2}$ be a piecewise smooth path in the $xy$-plane, and let $-C$ be the curve consisting of the same points as $C$ but with the opposite orientation. \n\nFor line integrals with respect to $y,$ we have the following properties:\n\n- ${\\int }_{-C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y=-{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y$\n\n- ${\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y+{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y & ={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y+{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =-{\\int }_{-{C}_{1}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y+{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =-3+5 \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "057ed5af-875f-53e1-addf-521f93615e7d" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186426493}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186426491 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259672 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "55c54a1b-25be-5636-b467-f5ac1a9b52a6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257520} {:answer/id #uuid "90ca79dc-a39f-5a16-a0bb-06f5058a2325" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258059} {:answer/id #uuid "90fced00-d179-5243-b71b-5f2af240f1e8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "5+5\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258062} {:answer/id #uuid "f4d9b801-9124-53be-9f21-3845b38cdb84" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258919} {:answer/id #uuid "f8d92c55-a2da-5101-b6a0-b111b36b6752" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258966}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186258919} :answer-field/id #uuid "4daa3094-63d7-51fd-af1c-c3ff26a2ec4f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259167}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "6bbf9141-617f-5455-b7c6-ab9b0397e741" :question/math-academy-id "q-124336" :question/problem "![](images/cb/cbef822c90e494cbe75e4a0b4ef93b058fccdd0111ec997f13930b1dba44406d.png)\n\nCalculate ${\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}y,$ where $C={C}_{1}\\cup {C}_{2}$ is the path comprising of two line segments, as shown above." :question/worked-solution "The line integral of unity with respect to $y$ along a path $C$ is equal to the net change in $y$ between the terminal and initial points:\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}y={y}_{2}-{y}_{1},\n$$\n\nwhere ${y}_{2}$ is the $y$-coordinate of the terminal point, and ${y}_{1}$ is the $y$-coordinate of the initial point.\n\nIn our case, ${y}_{2}=0$ and ${y}_{1}=-1.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}y={y}_{2}-{y}_{1}=0-(-1)=1.\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "494b3b4e-680e-5d29-ad8a-21380b15d664"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186426492 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186426488 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "10710cf3-2461-52e0-8591-26504aac5b0e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186426476} {:answer/id #uuid "268e558d-6b3a-5fd2-8942-b57b576a8436" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\pi +4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186426478} {:answer/id #uuid "2abac835-c02a-57a9-ab86-5e5d2da016b2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186426479} {:answer/id #uuid "40b22bf4-a6c7-569e-8fd1-56a445471c22" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186426480} {:answer/id #uuid "70a9bb49-2c55-5047-8c58-bf5ec89dd003" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186426481}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186426481} :answer-field/id #uuid "00fb8aa7-71b4-5be7-ac8f-e5b17414c65f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186426486}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "15dd7515-4c6a-53a1-ac3c-ffc4846bc122" :question/math-academy-id "q-124328" :question/problem "![](images/d4/d4d60c547afdb873c30e6c5e1f70d616dc31ace79b482f37ae51ea4f036d5a42.png)\n\nCalculate ${∮}_{C}\\,\\text{d}x,$ where $C$ is the closed path along the quarter circle of \nradius $2,$ traversed once in the counterclockwise direction, as shown above." :question/worked-solution "The line integral of unity with respect to $x$ along a path $C$ is equal to the net change in $x$ between the terminal and initial points:\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}\\,\\text{d}x={x}_{2}-{x}_{1},\n$$\n\nwhere ${x}_{2}$ is the $x$-coordinate of the terminal point, and ${x}_{1}$ is the $x$-coordinate of the initial point.\n\nIn our case, the initial and terminal points are the same (since the path is closed and is traversed only once). Therefore, ${x}_{1}={x}_{2},$ and we have\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}\\,\\text{d}x=0.\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "60ff33e4-fe39-587a-9f58-4a9d2690c97f" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186426491}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186426493 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259650 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2271c2f3-6a0e-52db-a073-7c6e91fd8bbd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257064} {:answer/id #uuid "7a614516-aa62-5836-8581-97092175214f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257865} {:answer/id #uuid "7f262654-5960-5b84-bd60-37392d856cee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257916} {:answer/id #uuid "f2324693-6303-570a-a9ea-853d9ea6b405" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{3\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258889} {:answer/id #uuid "f35b33e2-0c6e-55fd-902a-35519905732a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258903}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186257916} :answer-field/id #uuid "a181e6a4-7e9b-5c76-a47e-43a0425e674b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259316}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "5026f273-e6ca-5d94-86d8-dd1bd2a406be" :question/math-academy-id "q-124281" :question/problem "![](images/6b/6b095a29b433107c37cd7e28c853d9d3d3547fb64a9e874783206b86c0aca6c1.png)\n\nCalculate ${\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}x,$ where $C$ is the path along a quarter circle of radius $3,$ traversed from the point $(1,-3)$ to the point $(4,0),$ shown above." :question/worked-solution "The line integral of unity with respect to $x$ along a path $C$ is equal to the net change in $x$ between the terminal and initial points:\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}x={x}_{2}-{x}_{1},\n$$\n\nwhere ${x}_{2}$ is the $x$-coordinate of the terminal point, and ${x}_{1}$ is the $x$-coordinate of the initial point.\n\nIn our case, ${x}_{2}=4$ and ${x}_{1}=1.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}x={x}_{2}-{x}_{1}=4-1=3.\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "6fc8bd4b-f8e0-533e-a614-47a2e4b20930" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186426494}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186426494 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186426489 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1832c026-532a-5b68-9cf9-1e99d898f97f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\pi +3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186426477} {:answer/id #uuid "77030057-915a-5bf2-b853-884b00d3a0fe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186426482} {:answer/id #uuid "a4fa88c2-055c-5698-ac15-9fab33508e53" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186426483} {:answer/id #uuid "aa2086cd-91f8-5e89-a8cf-09b5f2f54c73" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\pi +3\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186426484} {:answer/id #uuid "c85b8a30-49c9-5708-b7f3-202e64369872" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186426485}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186426482} :answer-field/id #uuid "39828f92-e829-5bba-b1e4-9d6adfcd1f10" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186426487}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "b6d2ac1d-75ed-5453-8dc7-92123acc1f15" :question/math-academy-id "q-124308" :question/problem "![](images/fb/fb482ddbb5bde83cfd6ed383b0f321cb27377f34aafcb9e74e44d91824dcf366.png)\n\nCalculate ${\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}x,$ where $C={C}_{1}\\cup {C}_{2}$ is the path comprising of a line segment and a semicircle of radius $1,$ as shown above." :question/worked-solution "The line integral of unity with respect to $x$ along a path $C$ is equal to the net change in $x$ between the terminal and initial points:\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}x={x}_{2}-{x}_{1},\n$$\n\nwhere ${x}_{2}$ is the $x$-coordinate of the terminal point, and ${x}_{1}$ is the $x$-coordinate of the initial point.\n\nIn our case, ${x}_{2}=-3$ and ${x}_{1}=2.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}x={x}_{2}-{x}_{1}=(-3)-2=-5.\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "c27ffad8-d3ca-5837-bf61-af8fcb5c79a8" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186426492}}] :activity/title "Properties of Line Integrals With Respect to X and Y" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186426475}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046660} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186482637} :activity/id #uuid "faea1c30-e59b-555e-986e-d9c6a2962d7e" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050687 :topic/id #uuid "5f0fbf1a-01fe-5c95-8b44-55eb40e24c08" :topic/math-academy-id 3708} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186482635 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259610 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "126a7cd4-67ec-5bc6-9f63-b9d15c490f12" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256950} {:answer/id #uuid "2b62af94-63d3-5090-9678-72c681d50a7f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "Impossible to determine" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257169} {:answer/id #uuid "5e3a6519-cd2f-52db-a411-de74ed8ed855" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "11" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257607} {:answer/id #uuid "d06a24d7-7dbc-5df1-a807-98282f9c0371" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-11" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258592} {:answer/id #uuid "d1649fec-ac63-518f-a8e9-8c8b164b60dc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258607}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256950} :answer-field/id #uuid "49627605-3fb4-5b44-9d52-a141068215f8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259161}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "41e596da-5347-5fcf-affc-7dc004feff07" :question/math-academy-id "q-124181" :question/problem "If ${\\int }_{-{C}_{1}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y=8$ and ${\\int }_{-{C}_{2}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y=-3,$ calculate ${\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y$ given that $C={C}_{1}\\cup {C}_{2}.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $C={C}_{1}\\cup {C}_{2}$ be a piecewise smooth path in the $xy$-plane, and let $-C$ be the curve consisting of the same points as $C$ but with the opposite orientation. \n\nFor line integrals with respect to $y,$ we have the following properties:\n\n- ${\\int }_{-C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y=-{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y$\n\n- ${\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y+{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y & ={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y+{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =-{\\int }_{-{C}_{1}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y-{\\int }_{-{C}_{2}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =-8-(-3) \\\\  & =-5.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "664817ed-1c5d-56c6-9317-1db891f36e84" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186482649}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186482637 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259609 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1e6b1eff-f968-5af6-ab7e-497c5b252f54" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257032} {:answer/id #uuid "65fa06b4-0249-5df6-91c8-d1c80fb913d7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257681} {:answer/id #uuid "6f4f8d99-5f81-5776-b928-ceb7dbffd985" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257760} {:answer/id #uuid "ef1a5375-4524-5e93-b903-4a134bf9563e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "Impossible to determine" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258856} {:answer/id #uuid "fc6782be-6222-5451-a953-3a91a661ea0c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258989}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186257760} :answer-field/id #uuid "2af5069a-1d91-5111-8f4b-2d7bfb92501a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259109}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "f705291d-0bfe-5456-9170-c496d39786bb" :question/math-academy-id "q-124180" :question/problem "If ${\\int }_{-{C}_{1}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x=-1$ and ${\\int }_{-{C}_{2}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x=4,$ calculate ${\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x$ given that $C={C}_{1}\\cup {C}_{2}.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $C={C}_{1}\\cup {C}_{2}$ be a piecewise smooth path in the $xy$-plane, and let $-C$ be the curve consisting of the same points as $C$ but with the opposite orientation. \n\nFor line integrals with respect to $x,$ we have the following properties:\n\n- ${\\int }_{-C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x=-{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x$\n\n- ${\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-{\\int }_{-{C}_{1}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x-{\\int }_{-{C}_{2}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-(-1)-4 \\\\  & =-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "6f10ce92-f069-526b-924f-aad5da92df4d" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186482635}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186482649 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186415680 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6f835e91-ee38-5342-a3cd-ebbb06574004" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415643} {:answer/id #uuid "766165cf-8d6e-5c03-9b98-c79e31ebe685" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415645} {:answer/id #uuid "9af8db32-4cb8-5c67-bcff-3fbc9c9e8d60" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-4\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415652} {:answer/id #uuid "a4d3a26a-ce31-5aa6-94a6-091609c41ba6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415653} {:answer/id #uuid "e3f1e8c9-e884-5eab-b5ad-5240ff9dc466" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415664}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186415645} :answer-field/id #uuid "47d1b44e-be04-5aae-bd0c-c0fa80e8a07b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415673}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "a19a3e84-b3b7-545b-90bc-54d947e5986c" :question/math-academy-id "q-124300" :question/problem "![](images/71/71620d1e3e6691ef6c91382665fd258891984b1e32aeaec122663d65c1ca5b1b.png)\n\nCalculate ${\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}x,$ where $C$ is the path along the line segment between the points $(-3,-1)$ to the point $(1,3),$ as shown above." :question/worked-solution "The line integral of unity with respect to $x$ along a path $C$ is equal to the net change in $x$ between the terminal and initial points:\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}x={x}_{2}-{x}_{1},\n$$\n\nwhere ${x}_{2}$ is the $x$-coordinate of the terminal point, and ${x}_{1}$ is the $x$-coordinate of the initial point.\n\nIn our case, ${x}_{2}=1$ and ${x}_{1}=-3.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}x={x}_{2}-{x}_{1}=1-(-3)=4.\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "c50e5f01-04d4-55ae-95c5-9580fdda17a4" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186482655}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186482652 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259671 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "222f7372-478c-5a27-a063-8b07e8f92f6d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257061} {:answer/id #uuid "260fe7e6-dcde-5d6f-bd01-230ec63c06ad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257111} {:answer/id #uuid "457aa27e-2b18-5b49-aa06-db270cc7943d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257390} {:answer/id #uuid "81299222-742a-5d9d-a15d-9944b4e165e2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257930} {:answer/id #uuid "a82500ae-ffed-5208-83ef-1952b452cd21" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258250}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186257111} :answer-field/id #uuid "c408b8b1-0a44-526d-8f26-98e22333e00c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259374}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "9f388a61-d9fb-5445-8bd4-8cd72f371aff" :question/math-academy-id "q-124335" :question/problem "![](images/96/963f8812eb8226617c7d893ebdf675747ebed12c92fc6369de2b153a1c2e3b58.png)\n\nCalculate ${\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}y,$ where $C$ is the path along the semicircle of radius $2,$ transversed from the point $(1,-2)$ to the point $(1,2),$ as shown above." :question/worked-solution "The line integral of unity with respect to $y$ along a path $C$ is equal to the net change in $y$ between the terminal and initial points:\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}y={y}_{2}-{y}_{1},\n$$\n\nwhere ${y}_{2}$ is the $y$-coordinate of the terminal point, and ${y}_{1}$ is the $y$-coordinate of the initial point.\n\nIn our case, ${y}_{2}=2$ and ${y}_{1}=-2.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}y={y}_{2}-{y}_{1}=2-(-2)=4.\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "d68c1073-788b-5de9-b32e-40a492159af1"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186482655 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259670 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0c2ec8d6-1a05-588c-997f-14cc9bfb0ab8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\sqrt{13}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256900} {:answer/id #uuid "2264d3f3-6ba4-5db9-9859-6e8df205672b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257063} {:answer/id #uuid "c0061eac-4125-5f88-8e22-704bb968ada8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258457} {:answer/id #uuid "ea54b8e9-c9db-5811-b209-6fceac58e8e3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258817} {:answer/id #uuid "efaf75dd-29d0-5bee-a63b-c74ce7854eaa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\sqrt{13}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258863}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186258457} :answer-field/id #uuid "59744985-2971-5b60-8561-10d1ce839641" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259183}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "19da757c-af27-523a-9d2f-9ce008944b1f" :question/math-academy-id "q-124334" :question/problem "![](images/9c/9c42d875a65a6b54ad7769180f5ec2e831772ad44a89ec913951da40c6d90514.png)\n\nCalculate ${\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}y,$ where $C$ is the path along the line segment between the points $(-3,0)$ to the point $(0,2),$ as shown above." :question/worked-solution "The line integral of unity with respect to $y$ along a path $C$ is equal to the net change in $y$ between the terminal and initial points:\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}y={y}_{2}-{y}_{1},\n$$\n\nwhere ${y}_{2}$ is the $y$-coordinate of the terminal point, and ${y}_{1}$ is the $y$-coordinate of the initial point.\n\nIn our case, ${y}_{2}=2$ and ${y}_{1}=0.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}y={y}_{2}-{y}_{1}=2-0=2.\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "e608e1df-6587-55b1-9e73-f808cc254e61" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186482652}}] :activity/title "Properties of Line Integrals With Respect to X and Y" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481772}] [{:activity/expected-seconds 1080.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180573} :activity/id #uuid "210826c6-78d3-519b-84ab-0d8eb2721083" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050766 :topic/id #uuid "64ed08fd-258d-5456-ab85-4c3d693a62d2" :topic/math-academy-id 3687} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180573 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180764 :tutorial/content "In this lesson, we'll discuss the concepts of **work** and **kinetic energy** and describe how these concepts are related. \n\nThe **work done by the force** $F=F(x)$ in moving a particle from the position $x=x_1$ to the position $x=x_2$ along the $x$-axis is defined as\n\n$$\nW = \\int_{x_1}^{x_2} F(x) \\,\\textrm{d}x.\n$$\n\nWhen the force and distance are measured in Newtons ($\\textrm{N}$) and meters ($\\textrm{m}$), respectively, the work done by the force is measured in Joules ($\\rm{J}$).\n\nFor example, suppose a particle $P$ is moving along the $x$-axis under the action of a force of magnitude $4x^3\\,\\textrm{N},$ acting in the direction of increasing $x,$ as shown below. Distances along the $x$-axis are measured in meters.\n\n![](images/3f/3f0040d7a71563304cb954908988c82df1774a1197a5ce16c74a3b8ac65e04a0.png)\n\n \n\nLet's calculate the work done by the force in moving the particle from $x=1$ to $x=2.$\n\nSubstituting the given values into our formula, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}4{x}^{3}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={x}^{4}\\,{|}_{1}^{2} \\\\  & =16-1 \\\\  & =15\\,J.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the work done by $F$ in moving $P$ from $x_1=1\\,\\textrm m$ to $x_2=2\\,\\textrm m$ equals $15\\,\\textrm J.$\n\nWe can use the concept of work in conjunction with Newton's second law. Let's see an example." :tutorial/id #uuid "33cf6fa3-5f8d-579b-990b-ddeb9d1e3b8a" :tutorial/math-academy-id 7799 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "fa2ce148-bfaa-55f3-a775-56631c5bd980" :step/math-academy-id 45122 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180574}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180574 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179777 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179438 :question/id #uuid "622bd0ff-d08a-5504-8b38-8bc388bfcbf5" :question/math-academy-id "e-12525"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9696b3f4-42c8-5796-be3a-6c59c9600c95" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049423} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050723}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180099} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180100} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284403} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284405} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284407} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186389449} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186416257} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186416261} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186416262} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186416267} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186427393}] :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Work Done by a Force"} :step/id #uuid "cb90c47f-c22d-5b7f-a72a-cfc50bb4fdcd" :step/math-academy-id 45123 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180578}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180575 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179778 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179439 :question/id #uuid "4e428ee4-0017-5e83-887c-26e723b4af08" :question/math-academy-id "e-12526"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ec390427-7510-57ea-aedf-a930284f93b5" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049947}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180101} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180102} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284412} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284413} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284414} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186389463} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186389470} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186416258} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186416259} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186416264} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186416270}] :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Kinetic Energy of a Particle"} :step/id #uuid "54dddd69-accb-5eb8-beac-e9f9dbc87bf7" :step/math-academy-id 45124 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180579}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180576 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179779 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179440 :question/id #uuid "39eab788-1a8d-51af-b535-594cacc8ec2e" :question/math-academy-id "e-12527"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "94904fa2-bf02-5486-950c-979a4d12998e" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180103} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180105} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284400} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284401} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284402} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186376131} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186389428} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186389435} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186389442} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186416260} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186416263} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186416268}] :knowledge-point/title "Applying the Work-Energy Principle To Find the Work Done by a Force"} :step/id #uuid "496c1449-0914-5643-b8b4-95ec09fb5867" :step/math-academy-id 45125 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180577}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180577 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179780 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179441 :question/id #uuid "8f79d7e9-377f-5012-a762-279839fc806a" :question/math-academy-id "e-12528"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "793154ca-29f0-5552-97cd-57acbc915966" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048187}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180106} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180107} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284404} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284406} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284408} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284409} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284410} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284411} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186389456} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186416265} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186416266} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186416269}] :knowledge-point/title "Applying the Work-Energy Principle to Find a Particle's Velocity"} :step/id #uuid "b02a7662-acf4-57da-98d7-bae7093076f2" :step/math-academy-id 45126} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180578 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180765 :tutorial/content "The **kinetic energy** of a particle with mass $m$ and velocity $v$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\text{KE} = \\dfrac12mv^2.\n$$\n\nWhen the mass and velocity are measured in kilograms ($\\rm{kg}$) and meters per second ($\\rm{m/s}$), respectively, the particle's kinetic energy is measured in Joules ($\\rm{J}$). \n\nFor example, suppose a particle $P$ of mass $5\\,\\rm{kg}$ is moving along the $x$-axis from rest at the origin with an acceleration of $3t^2\\,\\rm{m/s}^2,$ where $t \\geq 0$ is the time in seconds. Let's calculate the particle's kinetic energy at time $t=2\\,\\rm{s}.$\n\nFirst, we find $v(t)$ by integrating the acceleration with respect to time:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\int a(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ v(t) & =\\int 3{t}^{2}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ v(t) & ={t}^{3}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo determine $C,$ we use the fact that $v(0)=0.$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n0 = 0^3 + C\n\\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad\nC = 0.\n$$\n\nHence, the velocity of the particle at time $t$ is $v(t) = t^3.$ In particular, its velocity at time $t=2\\,\\rm{s}$ is\n\n$$\nv=v(2) = 2^3 = 8\\,\\rm{m/s}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the kinetic energy of the particle at $t=2$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{KE} & =\\frac{1}{2}m{v}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 5\\cdot {8}^{2} \\\\  & =160\\,J.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote the following:\n\n- Kinetic energy is a *scalar* quantity.\n\n- You probably noticed that kinetic energy and work have the same units. This is not a coincidence! We'll learn more about that shortly.\n\n- In practical applications, it's often more important to calculate a *change* in the kinetic energy of a particle between two points in time. \n\nThe **change in kinetic energy** of a particle with mass $m,$ **initial velocity** $v_0,$ and **final velocity** $v_1$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\Delta \\, \\text{KE} = \\dfrac12mv_1^2 - \\dfrac12mv_0^2.\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "42f83ae3-3805-546b-a5bf-264b8b56d6ef" :tutorial/math-academy-id 7811 :tutorial/title "Kinetic Energy"} :step/id #uuid "3bec1351-7ee4-5f29-8760-5dd679edda2e" :step/math-academy-id 45179 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180575}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180579 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180766 :tutorial/content "The **work-energy principle** states that the work done by a resultant force on a particle equals the change in the particle's kinetic energy. That is,\n\n$$\nW = \\Delta\\, \\text{KE}\n$$\n\nwhere the change in kinetic energy of a particle is given by\n\n$$\n\\Delta\\, \\text{KE} = \\dfrac12 m v_1^2 - \\dfrac12 m v_0^2\n$$\n\nand $m$ is the particle's mass, $v_0$ is its initial velocity, and $v_1$ is its final velocity." :tutorial/id #uuid "e00f462b-c087-5c42-afc7-bfeffad5906b" :tutorial/math-academy-id 7812 :tutorial/title "The Work-Energy Principle"} :step/id #uuid "4d3d3f00-8ddb-5882-984a-2f48d2b19da6" :step/math-academy-id 45180 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180576}}] :activity/title "The Work-Energy Principle" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186179842}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046660} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186427396} :activity/id #uuid "595c8569-5220-5289-addf-624ae85604ef" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050766 :topic/id #uuid "64ed08fd-258d-5456-ab85-4c3d693a62d2" :topic/math-academy-id 3687} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186427394 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186389449 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "eb4d826c-14ba-585c-aebd-1facb3368012" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{4}\\,J" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389451} {:answer/id #uuid "8ab762ed-0112-5d52-b62f-05b3b484b0b7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}\\,J" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389452} {:answer/id #uuid "6aa053a0-465d-5f0b-8742-c3a588a86e3e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\,J" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389453} {:answer/id #uuid "ee6add72-368a-56d1-a701-8e8fbe0ceee5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}\\,J" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389454} {:answer/id #uuid "04400f5c-b01f-5a74-aa41-074c903d6463" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3\\,J" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389455}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186389451} :answer-field/id #uuid "7fc72668-2f1d-508f-943f-15caead3623c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389450}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "d31a00a5-c404-50d8-b7f2-fda34cf2cc53" :question/math-academy-id "q-163685" :question/problem "A particle $P$ moves along the $x$-axis under the action of a force of magnitude $\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{N}$ acting in the direction of increasing $x.$ Given that $x$ is measured in meters, calculate the work done by the force in moving the particle from $x=1$ to $x=4.$" :question/worked-solution "The work done by a force $F=F(x)$ in moving a particle from $x={x}_{1}$ to $x={x}_{2}$ is given by\n\n$$\nW={\\int }_{{x}_{1}}^{{x}_{2}}F(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nWhen the force and distance are measured in Newtons and meters, respectively, the work done by the force is measured in Joules ($J$).\n\nSubstituting the given values into our integral, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W & ={\\int }_{1}^{4}\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{x}\\,{|}_{1}^{4} \\\\  & =-(\\frac{1}{4}-1) \\\\  & =-(-\\frac{3}{4}) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{4}\\,J.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "593e6b92-f812-5412-8a5a-9a03f302bd99"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186427395 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186427393 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "005f40cc-1636-56ab-b246-5737a11e79cd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "5\\,J" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186427387} {:answer/id #uuid "49730251-5fc6-518d-bdf0-26723df97b08" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{9}{4}\\,J" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186427388} {:answer/id #uuid "5e6cfef4-3fa0-536d-accc-16bd9fddb64c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "7\\,J" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186427389} {:answer/id #uuid "a1bab12d-028f-5c7b-8260-e0dd0f56b76b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{15}{2}\\,J" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186427390} {:answer/id #uuid "ce34ff78-907d-5c9a-b016-26b849102736" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{20}{3}\\,J" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186427391}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186427391} :answer-field/id #uuid "7df70823-9962-541f-9d3b-9f6bc58730dc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186427392}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "720a885d-9cf4-5861-8b99-013e8190ec62" :question/math-academy-id "q-163683" :question/problem "A particle $P$ moves along the $x$-axis under the action of a force of magnitude $(2x+{x}^{2})\\,\\text{N}$ acting in the direction of increasing $x.$ Given that $x$ is measured in meters, calculate the work done by the force in moving the particle from $x=0$ to $x=2.$" :question/worked-solution "The work done by a force $F=F(x)$ in moving a particle from $x={x}_{1}$ to $x={x}_{2}$ is given by\n\n$$\nW={\\int }_{{x}_{1}}^{{x}_{2}}F(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nWhen the force and distance are measured in Newtons and meters, respectively, the work done by the force is measured in Joules ($J$).\n\nSubstituting the given values into our integral, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}(2x+{x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[{x}^{2}+\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{3}]}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & ={2}^{2}+\\frac{1}{3}({2}^{3})-({0}^{2}+\\frac{1}{3}({0}^{3})) \\\\  & =4+\\frac{8}{3}-0 \\\\  & =\\frac{20}{3}\\,J.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "6b7802f0-312d-58fa-a3d7-a69103bfe77b" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186427394}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186427396 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180103 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "452dcb36-4e8e-5c4d-bbab-d59aa1427bbf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4\\,J" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279210} {:answer/id #uuid "675b5d28-9c02-5ed8-b190-60068bfd7200" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "16\\,J" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279529} {:answer/id #uuid "7bcd8992-c86f-5ed8-8b85-3b006eb23206" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "8\\,J" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279696} {:answer/id #uuid "98b9878b-6e3c-5263-8d0a-a4133c54e84a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "12\\,J" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279941} {:answer/id #uuid "f3589965-bf1c-56f3-9f99-d2824d583527" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "10\\,J" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280718}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186279941} :answer-field/id #uuid "3768d6e3-78a3-5ed2-b58b-bc35eeb1c01e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280933}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "c92a85dc-fea5-582a-a4d0-c749606f94b3" :question/math-academy-id "q-163601" :question/problem "A particle $P$ of mass $2\\,kg$ moves along the $x$-axis under the action of a resultant force. The particle's velocity is $2t\\,m/s,$ where $t\\ge 0$ is the time in seconds. Calculate the work done by the force between the times $t=1\\,s$ and $t=2\\,s.$" :question/worked-solution "The work-energy principle states that the work done by a resultant force on a particle equals the change in the particle's kinetic energy. That is,\n\n$$\nW=Δ\\,\\text{KE}\n$$\n\nwhere the change in kinetic energy of a particle is given by\n\n$$\nΔ\\,\\text{KE}=\\frac{1}{2}m{v}_{1}^{2}-\\frac{1}{2}m{v}_{0}^{2}\n$$\n\nand $m$ is the particle's mass, ${v}_{0}$ is its initial velocity, and ${v}_{1}$ is its final velocity.\n\nThe particle's velocity is given by $v(t)=2t,$ where $t$ is time in seconds. So, the particle's initial velocity, at $t=1$, is\n\n$$\n{v}_{0}=v(1)=2(1)=2\\,m/s,\n$$\n\nand final velocity, at $t=2$, is\n\n$$\n{v}_{1}=v(2)=2(2)=4\\,m/s.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by the work-energy principle, the work done by the force between these times is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W & =Δ\\,\\text{KE} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 2\\cdot (4)^{2}-\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 2\\cdot (2)^{2} \\\\  & =16-4 \\\\  & =12\\,J.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "fa49f09b-6aed-5e72-bb78-032089ad9e8e" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186427395}}] :activity/title "The Work-Energy Principle" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186427386}] [{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134817} :activity/id #uuid "3a450e6c-560e-5c98-a021-778f5de2c088" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050841 :topic/id #uuid "6aced1c3-bd0e-5b1d-973e-105b955f9e1a" :topic/math-academy-id 2126} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134817 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186135105 :tutorial/content "Let $V$ be an inner product space with the inner product $\\langle\\cdot, \\cdot\\rangle.$ If \n\n$$\n\\big\\{ \\mathbf{a}_1, \\mathbf{a}_2 , \\ldots, \\mathbf{a}_n \\big\\}\n$$\n\n is a set of mutually orthogonal vectors in $V,$  then the **orthogonal projection** of a vector $\\mathbf{x}\\in V$ onto the subspace \n\n$$\nS = \\textrm{Span}\\{ \\mathbf{a}_1, \\mathbf{a}_2 , \\ldots, \\mathbf{a}_n \\}\n$$\n\nwith respect to the inner product is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x & ={\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{1}}x+{\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{2}}x+⋯+{\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{n}}x \\\\  & =\\frac{⟨x,{a}_{1}⟩}{⟨{a}_{1},{a}_{1}⟩}{a}_{1}+\\frac{⟨x,{a}_{2}⟩}{⟨{a}_{2},{a}_{2}⟩}{a}_{2}+⋯+\\frac{⟨x,{a}_{n}⟩}{⟨{a}_{n},{a}_{n}⟩}{a}_{n}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn other words, the orthogonal projection of $\\mathbf{x}$ onto $S$ is the sum of the orthogonal projections of $\\mathbf{x}$ onto each one-dimensional subspace $\\textrm{Span}\\big\\{ \\mathbf{a}_i\\}$ for $i=1, 2, \\cdots, n.$ \n\nNote the following:\n\n- This is entirely analogous to the corresponding formula for Euclidean spaces.\n\n- As in Euclidean spaces, the formula works *only* when the set of vectors $\\{ \\mathbf{a}_1, \\mathbf{a}_2 , \\ldots, \\mathbf{a}_n \\}$ is orthogonal with respect to the inner product!" :tutorial/id #uuid "9b462101-527b-5e26-bcd4-89b8459552bd" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5162 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "91e83dab-899c-5474-8059-ebdcc7a5278a" :step/math-academy-id 32457 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134885}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134818 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133873 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133689 :question/id #uuid "18068394-b64b-5bc8-a2da-b0db4085ecba" :question/math-academy-id "e-8729"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "df300955-830a-5347-9aeb-2b2b25f865c8" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048169} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048893}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134060} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134563} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186240902} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186240903} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186240904} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186240948} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186240949} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186240961} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186240962}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Orthogonal Projection of a Vector Onto a One-Dimensional Subspace"} :step/id #uuid "4240c631-050e-58b5-968f-46d616a5f794" :step/math-academy-id 32458 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134819}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134819 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133874 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133690 :question/id #uuid "4e4a5db0-2ea0-542e-abdd-c5b3abfe2134" :question/math-academy-id "e-8730"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "39692b40-7d68-5ae5-9fe1-0f8b0b2e0d77" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048495} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050009}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134064} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134562} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186240950} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186240955} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186240978} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186240983} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186240989} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186240996} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186241003} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186241014} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186241046} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186241049}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Orthogonal Projection of a Vector Onto a Subspace Spanned by Orthogonal Vectors"} :step/id #uuid "e60ded4d-fa5d-5393-8f16-7c1cc6abe2dd" :step/math-academy-id 32459 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134820}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134820 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186135106 :tutorial/content "Suppose that $V$ is a vector space equipped with an inner product $\\left\\langle\\cdot, \\cdot\\right\\rangle$ and that $\\mathbf{v}, \\mathbf{w} \\in V.$ The **distance $d$ between $\\mathbf v$ and $\\mathbf w$ with respect to the inner product** is defined by the *norm* of $\\mathbf{v}- \\mathbf{w}.$ That is,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & =‖v-w‖=\\sqrt{⟨v-w,v-w⟩}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor example, suppose that $V = \\mathcal{C}[0,1],$ the vector space of continuous functions over $[0,1],$ equipped with the inner product\n\n$$\n\\langle x(t),y(t) \\rangle = \\int_0^{1} x(t) y(t) \\, \\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nThe distance between the functions $f(t)=4t$ and $g(t)=t+1$ with respect to the given inner product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & =‖f(t)-g(t)‖ \\\\  & =‖4t-(t+1)‖ \\\\  & =‖3t-1‖ \\\\  & =\\sqrt{⟨3t-1,3t-1⟩} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{\\int }_{0}^{1}(3t-1)^{2}\\,\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{\\frac{1}{9}\\cdot (3t-1)^{3}|}_{0}^{1}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{1}{9}\\cdot [(8)-(-1)]} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{1}{9}\\cdot 9} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{1} \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMore generally, given an orthogonal set \n\n$$\n\\big\\{ \\mathbf{a}_1, \\mathbf{a}_2 , \\ldots, \\mathbf{a}_n \\big\\} \\subset  V\n$$\n\nand a vector $\\mathbf x \\in V,$  the **distance between $\\mathbf{x}$ and the subspace** \n\n$$\nS=\\textrm{Span}\\{ \\mathbf{a}_1,\\mathbf{a}_2,  \\ldots, \\mathbf{a}_n \\}\n$$\n\nis defined as\n\n$$\n\\Vert{ \\color{black} \\mathbf{x} - \\text{proj}_{S}\\,\\mathbf{x} }\\Vert.\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "4ca26c36-a2e4-51d5-ae7d-a4b59234cedb" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5163 :tutorial/title "The Distance Between a Vector and a Subspace"} :step/id #uuid "7d1a7540-bb8b-5865-a0e0-852beed15e26" :step/math-academy-id 32460 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134821}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134821 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133875 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133691 :question/id #uuid "d7541321-4042-54af-9b64-59f255a6f1a3" :question/math-academy-id "e-8733"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "25e84fac-02c3-5111-98b3-bc67fe1ec9ab" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049274}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134564} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134565} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186241059} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186241063} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186241065} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186241066} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186241068} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186241070} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186241072} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186241074} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186375652}] :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Distance Between a Vector and a Subspace"} :step/id #uuid "20a627a4-468b-5c73-a4aa-e3ab9c87207e" :step/math-academy-id 32485 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134828}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134828 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186135107 :tutorial/content "The orthogonal projection of a vector $\\mathbf{x}\\in V$ onto $\\textrm{Span}\\{\\mathbf{a}\\}$ (or simply onto a vector $\\mathbf{a}$) is the vector $\\text{proj}_{\\mathbf{a}}\\,\\mathbf{x}\\in \\textrm{Span}\\{\\mathbf{a}\\}$ that is \"closest\" to $\\mathbf{x}$ with respect to the distance defined by the inner product.\n\nAlthough we've considered abstract vector spaces with any possible inner product in this lesson, we can still visualize this situation with the following diagram:\n\n![](images/09/099a0bb20552824d2376994c3eb7145e48bbf78552d16de39fec44bda6b766f2.png)\n\nAs shown in the diagram, the projection of $\\mathbf{x}$ onto $\\textrm{Span}\\{\\mathbf{a}\\}$ is the vector $\\text{proj}_{\\mathbf{a}}\\,\\mathbf{x}$ such that $\\mathbf{x} - \\text{proj}_{\\mathbf{a}}\\,\\mathbf{x}$ is orthogonal to $\\textrm{Span}\\{\\mathbf{a}\\}.$\n\nFurthermore, $\\| \\mathbf{x} - \\text{proj}_{\\mathbf{a}}\\,\\mathbf{x} \\|$ will be the smallest possible distance (with respect to our inner product) from $\\mathbf{x}$ to any vector in $\\textrm{Span}\\{\\mathbf{a}\\}.$" :tutorial/id #uuid "629bc20f-76c3-5217-b763-e4ab0c831a8d" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5343 :tutorial/title "The Intuition Behind Orthogonal Projections in Inner Product Spaces"} :step/id #uuid "97501e93-7a73-5f78-8d81-1f3d7a340746" :step/math-academy-id 33420} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134885 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186135141 :tutorial/content "Consider the functions \n\n$$\nf_1(t)=t,\\qquad f_2(t)=t^2+1, \\qquad h(t)= 5t^3,\n$$\n\nin the vector space $V = \\mathcal{C}[-1,1]$ of continuous functions over $[-1,1],$ where the inner product is defined as\n\n$$\n\\langle x(t),y(t) \\rangle = \\int_{-1}^1 x(t) y(t) \\, \\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nLet's find the orthogonal projection of $h(t)$ onto the subspace\n\n$$\nS= \\textrm{Span}\\{f_1(t), f_2(t)\\},\n$$\n\n \n\ngiven that $f_1(t) \\perp f_2(t)$ with respect to this inner product. \n\nSince the set $\\{f_1(t),f_2(t)\\}$ is orthogonal, the orthogonal projection of $h(t)$ onto $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\textrm{proj}_{S}\\,h(t) \n= \\textrm{proj}_{f_1(t)}\\,h(t) + \\textrm{proj}_{f_2(t)}\\,h(t).\n$$\n\nLet's now compute these projections:\n\n- First, we compute  the orthogonal projection of $h(t)$ onto $\\textrm{Span}\\{f_1(t)\\}\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{{f}_{1}(t)}\\,h(t) & =\\frac{⟨h(t),{f}_{1}(t)⟩}{⟨{f}_{1}(t),{f}_{1}(t)⟩}\\,{f}_{1}(t) \\\\  & =\\frac{{\\int }_{-1}^{1}5{t}^{4}\\,\\text{d}t}{{\\int }_{-1}^{1}{t}^{2}\\,\\text{d}t}\\cdot t \\\\  & =3t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Next, we compute  the orthogonal projection of $h(t)$ onto $\\textrm{Span}\\{f_2(t)\\}\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{{f}_{2}(t)}\\,h(t) & =\\frac{⟨h(t),{f}_{2}(t)⟩}{⟨{f}_{2}(t),{f}_{2}(t)⟩}\\,{f}_{2}(t) \\\\  & =\\frac{{\\int }_{-1}^{1}5{t}^{3}({t}^{2}+1)\\,\\text{d}t}{{\\int }_{-1}^{1}({t}^{2}+1)^{2}\\,\\text{d}t}\\cdot ({t}^{2}+1) \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote: We used the fact that the integrand in the numerator is an odd function, so its integral over $[-1,1]$ must be zero.\n\nFinally, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,h(t) & ={\\text{proj}}_{{f}_{1}(t)}\\,h(t)+{\\text{proj}}_{{f}_{2}(t)}\\,h(t) \\\\  & =3t+0 \\\\  & =3t.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "975c7894-a67f-5528-875e-2e55a94d99b9" :tutorial/math-academy-id 6130 :tutorial/title "A Worked Example"} :step/id #uuid "b89a94ac-0dd4-505c-b266-41783d527047" :step/math-academy-id 37245 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134818}}] :activity/title "Projecting Vectors Onto Subspaces in Inner Product Spaces" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186134005}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046660} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186413797} :activity/id #uuid "fba5b1da-9880-515c-af94-05681b35cd3b" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050841 :topic/id #uuid "6aced1c3-bd0e-5b1d-973e-105b955f9e1a" :topic/math-academy-id 2126} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186413793 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134064 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "00a97740-bf4e-5245-a223-1105ad76868c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238086} {:answer/id #uuid "0ccb496a-1f3e-5f9c-9be5-7756bb97a385" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238169} {:answer/id #uuid "9dbfd16e-b04d-5762-91cf-d69f6af7923d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239432} {:answer/id #uuid "a5578604-9539-5565-8bbb-579ff2d23c8a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239490} {:answer/id #uuid "ff74f82e-113d-5ba7-9d63-962e019ecfd4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186240320}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186238086} :answer-field/id #uuid "50e2f23b-8197-5ffd-805f-f3ecff761a73" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186240475}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "e7a8c44b-b300-5e66-9428-e03f400ebf5f" :question/math-academy-id "q-105416" :question/problem "Let $V=C[-\\pi ,\\pi ],$ the vector space of continuous functions over $[-\\pi ,\\pi ]$, and let the inner product on $V$ be defined as\n\n$$\n⟨x(t),y(t)⟩={\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }x(t)y(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nNow consider the following elements of $V:$\n\n$$\n{f}_{1}(t)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,{f}_{2}(t)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,g(t)={sin}^{2}⁡t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\n$$\n\nFind the orthogonal projection of $g(t)$ onto $S=\\text{Span}{{f}_{1}(t),{f}_{2}(t)}$ given that ${f}_{1}(t)⊥{f}_{2}(t)$ with respect to the inner product, and that the orthogonal projection of $g(t)$ onto $\\text{Span}{{f}_{2}(t)}$ equals $-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t.$" :question/worked-solution "Since the set ${{f}_{1}(t),{f}_{2}(t)}$ is orthogonal, the orthogonal projection of $g(t)$ onto the subspace $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,g(t)={\\text{proj}}_{{f}_{1}(t)}\\,g(t)+{\\text{proj}}_{{f}_{2}(t)}\\,g(t).\n$$\n\nWe are given that ${\\text{proj}}_{{f}_{2}(t)}\\,g(t)=-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,$ so let's now find the orthogonal projection of $g(t)$ onto $\\text{Span}{{f}_{1}(t)}.$ This can be computed as\n\n$$\n{\\text{proj}}_{{f}_{1}(t)}\\,g(t)=\\frac{⟨g(t),{f}_{1}(t)⟩}{⟨{f}_{1}(t),{f}_{1}(t)⟩}\\,{f}_{1}(t).\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨g(t),{f}_{1}(t)⟩ & ={\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }g(t){f}_{1}(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }({sin}^{2}⁡t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡t({sin}^{2}⁡t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\,\\text{d}t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that $\\operatorname{sin}⁡t$ is an odd function, while ${sin}^{2}⁡t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t$ is even. Hence, their product $\\operatorname{sin}⁡t({sin}^{2}⁡t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)$ must be an odd function. Now, since we have an integral over the interval $[-\\pi ,\\pi ],$ which is symmetric about the origin, we can conclude that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡t({sin}^{2}⁡t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\,\\text{d}t=0.\n$$\n\n  \n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n{\\text{proj}}_{{f}_{1}(t)}\\,g(t)=\\frac{0}{⟨{f}_{1}(t),{f}_{1}(t)⟩}\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡t=0.\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,g(t) & ={\\text{proj}}_{{f}_{1}(t)}\\,g(t)+{\\text{proj}}_{{f}_{2}(t)}\\,g(t) \\\\  & =0-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "0a412cdd-29f8-5273-ab55-2a8dce391dae" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186413795}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186413794 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186241003 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1a9db971-884b-5cf9-9a84-c91c9fa4fa01" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "8+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238276} {:answer/id #uuid "39658250-b0fe-59bb-a334-a4e517b0d75e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238550} {:answer/id #uuid "880b7d31-ec04-56db-ad2a-f754621f1b8d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239237} {:answer/id #uuid "b1b15087-e383-5255-8efc-84ad43c168b5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "6-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239607} {:answer/id #uuid "cd180c30-2f84-5a24-af62-8965f36db06d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239851}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186239607} :answer-field/id #uuid "51c982ce-3e72-5b74-bcb0-6eb543685681" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186240480}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "2bf55fb2-ca86-5707-a52a-d891899878f4" :question/math-academy-id "q-105186" :question/problem "Let $V=C[-\\pi ,\\pi ],$ the vector space of continuous functions over $[-\\pi ,\\pi ]$, and let the inner product on $V$ be defined as\n\n$$\n⟨x(t),y(t)⟩={\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }x(t)y(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nNow consider the following elements of $V:$\n\n$$\n{f}_{1}(t)=2,\\,{f}_{2}(t)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,g(t)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+6\n$$\n\nFind the orthogonal projection of $g(t)$ onto $S=\\text{Span}{{f}_{1}(t),{f}_{2}(t)}$ given that ${f}_{1}(t)⊥{f}_{2}(t)$ with respect to the inner product, and that the orthogonal projection of $g(t)$ onto $\\text{Span}{{f}_{1}(t)}$ equals $6.$" :question/worked-solution "Since the set ${{f}_{1}(t),{f}_{2}(t)}$ is orthogonal, the orthogonal projection of $g(t)$ onto the subspace $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,g(t)={\\text{proj}}_{{f}_{1}(t)}\\,g(t)+{\\text{proj}}_{{f}_{2}(t)}\\,g(t).\n$$\n\nWe are given that ${\\text{proj}}_{{f}_{1}(t)}\\,g(t)=6,$ so let's now find the orthogonal projection of $g(t)$ onto $\\text{Span}{{f}_{2}(t)}.$ This can be computed as\n\n$$\n{\\text{proj}}_{{f}_{2}(t)}\\,g(t)=\\frac{⟨g(t),{f}_{2}(t)⟩}{⟨{f}_{2}(t),{f}_{2}(t)⟩}\\,{f}_{2}(t).\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨g(t),{f}_{2}(t)⟩ & ={\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }g(t){f}_{2}(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+6)\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t-2{\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }{sin}^{2}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t+6{\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that $\\operatorname{sin}⁡t$ is an odd function, while $\\operatorname{cos}⁡t$ is even. Hence, their product $\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t$ must be an odd function. Now, since we have integrals over the interval $[-\\pi ,\\pi ],$ which is symmetric about the origin, we can conclude that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t={\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t=0.\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨g(t),{f}_{2}(t)⟩ & =-2{\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }{sin}^{2}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{{f}_{2}(t)}\\,g(t) & =\\frac{-2{\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }{sin}^{2}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t}{⟨{f}_{2}(t),{f}_{2}(t)⟩}\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\  & =\\frac{-2{\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }{sin}^{2}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t}{{\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }{sin}^{2}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\  & =-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,g(t) & ={\\text{proj}}_{{f}_{1}(t)}\\,g(t)+{\\text{proj}}_{{f}_{2}(t)}\\,g(t) \\\\  & =6-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "0bc7e18b-fdb5-5429-828f-e671a9abcbda" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186413793}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186413795 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186241059 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "694bbe7e-1033-5685-8db7-c579e340a4af" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238969} {:answer/id #uuid "7187a2ab-aec2-5516-9817-82c6b1e6f0ad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239028} {:answer/id #uuid "98f9f087-a4cb-5b2b-ba88-6c34977c1ab6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\sqrt{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239383} {:answer/id #uuid "d0b7eedf-e87f-53c1-a7c0-62ebe727a2fa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239882} {:answer/id #uuid "f69f76e8-1b93-5cd1-b517-2cdfc888efb3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186240229}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186239028} :answer-field/id #uuid "eabf99e8-310f-5528-b7c0-2d81c99774f8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186240742}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "a70f1b99-0d4b-5ef2-9e7e-0dc7f6d78417" :question/math-academy-id "q-105461" :question/problem "Let $V=C[0,1],$ the vector space of functions that are continuous over $[0,1].$ Given that the inner product on $V$ is defined as \n\n$$\n⟨x(t),y(t)⟩={\\int }_{0}^{1}x(t)y(t)\\,\\text{d}t,\n$$\n\nfind the distance between $f(t)=3t$ and $g(t)=-3t+3$ with respect to the inner product." :question/worked-solution "The distance between $f(t)=3t$ and $g(t)=-3t+3$ with respect to the given inner product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & =‖f(t)-g(t)‖ \\\\  & =‖3t-(-3t+3)‖ \\\\  & =‖6t-3‖ \\\\  & =\\sqrt{⟨6t-3,6t-3⟩}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the inner product, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨6t-3,6t-3⟩ & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(6t-3)^{2}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(36{t}^{2}-36t+9)\\text{d}t \\\\  & =[12{t}^{3}-18{t}^{2}+9t]_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =(12-18+9)-0 \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the distance is $d=\\sqrt{3}.$"} :step/id #uuid "5b37fdbb-b243-534e-930c-c4161c64569a" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186413796}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186413796 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186241063 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "10e4eb1a-f07b-5500-9b6b-bb104419f1ab" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238193} {:answer/id #uuid "1597005a-083a-5b2f-904c-d3601ce7fd24" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238235} {:answer/id #uuid "189191f2-31ca-5b6a-bb47-311abb8904ac" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238264} {:answer/id #uuid "46644984-af18-5419-96cd-d6307758e813" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238666} {:answer/id #uuid "94691432-7499-5d85-bd53-7c03102b3e45" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239340}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186238264} :answer-field/id #uuid "e7bf2326-7e5c-5bac-b442-4f7e564308dc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186240734}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "5d460b9a-6ed3-5d23-955f-f3e1840b55a2" :question/math-academy-id "q-105475" :question/problem "Let $V=C[0,1],$ the vector space of functions that are continuous over $[0,1].$ Given that the inner product on $V$ is defined as \n\n$$\n⟨x(t),y(t)⟩={\\int }_{0}^{1}x(t)y(t)\\,\\text{d}t,\n$$\n\nfind the distance between $f(t)=8t$ and the subspace spanned by $g(t)=4t-2$ with respect to the inner product." :question/worked-solution "The orthogonal projection of $f(t)$ onto $\\text{Span}{g(t)}$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{g(t)}\\,f(t) & =\\frac{⟨f(t),g(t)⟩}{⟨g(t),g(t)⟩}\\,g(t) \\\\  & =\\frac{{\\int }_{0}^{1}8t\\cdot (4t-2)\\,\\text{d}t}{{\\int }_{0}^{1}(4t-2)^{2}\\,\\text{d}t}\\cdot (4t-2) \\\\  & =\\frac{{\\int }_{0}^{1}(32{t}^{2}-16t)\\,\\text{d}t}{{\\int }_{0}^{1}(16{t}^{2}-16t+4)\\,\\text{d}t}\\cdot (4t-2) \\\\  & =\\frac{[\\frac{32}{3}{t}^{3}-8{t}^{2}]_{0}^{1}}{[\\frac{16}{3}{t}^{3}-8{t}^{2}+4t]_{0}^{1}}\\cdot (4t-2) \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{32}{3}-8)-0}{(\\frac{16}{3}-4)-0}\\cdot (6t-3) \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{8}{3})}{(\\frac{4}{3})}\\cdot (4t+2) \\\\  & =2\\cdot (4t-2) \\\\  & =8t-4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe distance between $f(t)=8t$ and the subspace spanned by $g(t)=4t-2$ with respect to our inner product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & =‖f(t)-{\\text{proj}}_{g(t)}\\,f(t)‖ \\\\  & =‖8t-(8t-4)‖ \\\\  & =‖4‖ \\\\  & =\\sqrt{⟨4,4⟩}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the inner product, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨4,4⟩ & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}4\\cdot 4\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}16\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =16t\\,{|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =16-0 \\\\  & =16.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the distance is $d=\\sqrt{16}=4.$"} :step/id #uuid "5b4d4a19-f8e8-5a9a-8c13-582c4e2b0863"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186413797 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186240983 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "09b29cd5-55b5-5722-ba90-23f769345723" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238150} {:answer/id #uuid "5bc19ba5-b098-5af2-b759-18b7d61fa0df" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238854} {:answer/id #uuid "64e8e822-ab38-5fcf-a7cb-162397992404" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238937} {:answer/id #uuid "9cd846ec-dcb9-5576-8a82-5cbd0e68c90d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239418} {:answer/id #uuid "a19047b8-6624-5f0c-b217-a2d6a5d4fcd6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+5\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239462}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186238937} :answer-field/id #uuid "f5a8c5d5-fcb2-5bdd-8555-14fa5c61c1d7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186240761}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "645a82fe-e42f-5dc9-9e79-d8bd0747a894" :question/math-academy-id "q-105136" :question/problem "Let $V=C[-\\pi ,\\pi ],$ the vector space of continuous functions over $[-\\pi ,\\pi ]$, and let the inner product on $V$ be defined as\n\n$$\n⟨x(t),y(t)⟩={\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }x(t)y(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nNow consider the following elements of $V:$\n\n$$\n{f}_{1}(t)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,{f}_{2}(t)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,g(t)=5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+1\n$$\n\nFind the orthogonal projection of $g(t)$ onto $S=\\text{Span}{{f}_{1}(t),{f}_{2}(t)}$ given that ${f}_{1}(t)⊥{f}_{2}(t)$ with respect to the inner product, and that the orthogonal projection of $g(t)$ onto $\\text{Span}{{f}_{1}(t)}$ equals $5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t.$" :question/worked-solution "Since the set ${{f}_{1}(t),{f}_{2}(t)}$ is orthogonal, the orthogonal projection of $g(t)$ onto the subspace $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,g(t)={\\text{proj}}_{{f}_{1}(t)}\\,g(t)+{\\text{proj}}_{{f}_{2}(t)}\\,g(t).\n$$\n\nWe are given that ${\\text{proj}}_{{f}_{1}(t)}\\,g(t)=5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,$ so let's now find the orthogonal projection of $g(t)$ onto $\\text{Span}{{f}_{2}(t)}.$ This can be computed as\n\n$$\n{\\text{proj}}_{{f}_{2}(t)}\\,g(t)=\\frac{⟨g(t),{f}_{2}(t)⟩}{⟨{f}_{2}(t),{f}_{2}(t)⟩}\\,{f}_{2}(t).\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨g(t),{f}_{2}(t)⟩ & ={\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }g(t){f}_{2}(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }(5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+1)\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =5{\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t-4{\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }{sin}^{2}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t+{\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that $\\operatorname{sin}⁡t$ is an odd function, while $\\operatorname{cos}⁡t$ is even. Hence, their product $\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t$ must be an odd function. Now, since we have an integral over the interval $[-\\pi ,\\pi ],$ which is symmetric about the origin, we can conclude that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t={\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t=0.\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨g(t),{f}_{2}(t)⟩ & =-4{\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }{sin}^{2}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{{f}_{2}(t)}\\,g(t) & =\\frac{-4{\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }{sin}^{2}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t}{⟨{f}_{2}(t),{f}_{2}(t)⟩}\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\  & =\\frac{-4{\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }{sin}^{2}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t}{{\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }{sin}^{2}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\  & =-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,g(t) & ={\\text{proj}}_{{f}_{1}(t)}\\,g(t)+{\\text{proj}}_{{f}_{2}(t)}\\,g(t) \\\\  & =5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "681a7c6c-5a02-51e9-b790-4836129a3fb4" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186413794}}] :activity/title "Projecting Vectors Onto Subspaces in Inner Product Spaces" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413792}] [{:activity/expected-seconds 420.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199401} :activity/id #uuid "5f56a4f9-bc1e-5b83-af00-1c3cf76a9018" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050910 :topic/id #uuid "708a8959-ec54-51e9-bf31-27b3539bdb74" :topic/math-academy-id 4319} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199401 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199537 :tutorial/content "A linear transformation $\\mathbf{Q}:\\mathbb{R}^n \\to \\mathbb{R}^n$ is an **orthogonal linear transformation** if for any $\\mathbf{x}, \\mathbf{y} \\in \\mathbb{R}^n,$ we have that\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{Q}(\\mathbf{x}) \\cdot \\mathbf{Q}(\\mathbf{y}) = \\mathbf{x} \\cdot \\mathbf{y}.\n$$\n\nIn this case, we say that $\\mathbf{Q}$ *preserves the dot product*.\n\nThe linear transformation $\\mathbf{Q}$ being orthogonal is equivalent to the following:\n\n- The matrix $Q$ of transformation $\\mathbf{Q}$ relative to any orthonormal basis (including the standard basis) of $\\mathbb{R}^n$ is orthogonal.\n\n- $\\| \\mathbf{Q}(\\mathbf{x}) \\| = \\| \\mathbf{x} \\|$. In other words, $\\mathbf{Q}$ preserves vector norms.\n\n- If $\\mathbf{x} \\perp \\mathbf{y}$ then $\\mathbf{Q}(\\mathbf{x}) \\perp \\mathbf{Q}(\\mathbf{y})$ for any $\\mathbf{x}, \\mathbf{y} \\in \\mathbb{R}^n.$ In general, $\\mathbf{Q}$ preserves the angle between any two vectors.\n\n- $\\mathbf{Q}$ maps any orthogonal (orthonormal) set into an orthogonal (orthonormal) set." :tutorial/id #uuid "fed8f501-fcb5-5852-bf23-1e999e4c9e1e" :tutorial/math-academy-id 8098 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "1ef8589c-4324-58ea-9201-f5ba88997554" :step/math-academy-id 52322 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199402}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199402 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198448 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198131 :question/id #uuid "0070a453-0237-571f-84fc-a9f58446e6a9" :question/math-academy-id "e-12954"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "455b3713-34a3-535d-8baf-d279a0341e9f" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048849} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049045} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049606}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198978} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198979} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284757} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284758} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284759} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186326847} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186406654} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186406656} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186446169} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186485506} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186485507}] :knowledge-point/title "Applying the Angle Preserving Property of Orthogonal Linear Transformations"} :step/id #uuid "8416cf3f-5dd0-52a4-a389-a27f110ac992" :step/math-academy-id 52323 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199403}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199403 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198449 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198130 :question/id #uuid "a77db107-d157-5063-b44a-1ca0a93be099" :question/math-academy-id "e-12951"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b4a5d6b1-e76a-5b33-8973-04d1d9062dc6" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049811}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198749} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198750} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284748} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284761} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284764} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186406655} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186411865} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186415156} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186446168} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186485518} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186485595}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Orthogonality of a Two-Vector Set"} :step/id #uuid "49d42b8f-bd36-5613-9a00-8f0c28f5f77d" :step/math-academy-id 52326}] :activity/title "Orthogonal Linear Transformations" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186198524}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046673} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186406657} :activity/id #uuid "11d38e3f-6339-5429-8d89-8272fca9a35c" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050910 :topic/id #uuid "708a8959-ec54-51e9-bf31-27b3539bdb74" :topic/math-academy-id 4319} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186406657 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186406656 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "33d47db5-c434-55d6-9456-167ab8a0b67c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406641} {:answer/id #uuid "7c3a74ea-6a6d-5da3-b53b-157fcb966ead" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406643} {:answer/id #uuid "b81c5bc9-bfe5-5ce5-928d-fa684e207472" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406645} {:answer/id #uuid "c6a8591a-2779-5016-a0d3-d51746099b13" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406647} {:answer/id #uuid "f3f62bc1-8a13-5efa-ad9a-759018432b14" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "17" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406650}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186406641} :answer-field/id #uuid "ac075b6f-3299-5310-ad52-26f9cf648385" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406653}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "cf616da5-d6d0-5837-a9fb-64c2cf137040" :question/math-academy-id "q-40488" :question/problem "Let $Q$ be an orthogonal linear transformation. Find $Q(x)\\cdot Q(y)$, where $x=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]$ and $y=[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$" :question/worked-solution "Since $Q$ is orthogonal, we have that $Q(x)\\cdot Q(y)=x\\cdot y$ for any vectors $x$ and $y.$\n\nThe linear transformation $Q$ being orthogonal is equivalent to the following:\n\n- The matrix $Q$ of transformation $Q$ relative to any orthonormal basis (including the standard basis) of ${R}^{n}$ is orthogonal.\n\n- $||Q(x)||=||x||.$\n\n- If $x⊥y$ then $Q(x)⊥Q(y)$ for any $x,y\\in {R}^{n}.$ In general, $Q$ preserves the angle between any two vectors.\n\n- $Q$ maps any orthogonal (orthonormal) set into an orthogonal (orthonormal) set.\n\nIn this case, since $Q$ is orthogonal, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(x)\\cdot Q(y) & =x\\cdot y \\\\  & =1\\cdot (-3)+3\\cdot 4+(-2)\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =7.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "0bda9774-6623-5015-9c4f-2876bc204742" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186406658}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186406658 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186406655 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "047c19ef-c76a-57b3-b13e-81cac8438947" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473112} {:answer/id #uuid "0f411e21-0bfc-5e60-be10-aa9385c60d56" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473113}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186473113} :answer-field/id #uuid "41b5e6b2-6fa4-5699-91d3-98491ea01274" :answer-field/key "U" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473951} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3b5e99f8-6d7b-5605-8eeb-768c689a1a11" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473114} {:answer/id #uuid "d3e8b7c2-03e8-52a8-8c30-1f1597b0a406" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473115}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186473114} :answer-field/id #uuid "2909a2f7-876c-5e10-a289-6837f83679fc" :answer-field/key "V" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473952} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "829a1b56-ccd5-5381-aeca-6ba71098f21a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473116} {:answer/id #uuid "adcc7d05-7e81-525a-b650-a03adf317913" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473117}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186473117} :answer-field/id #uuid "51b28bdf-55b7-5470-b1f6-bd8182431d5f" :answer-field/key "W" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473953}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "9892c6c7-24de-57f2-a3e8-0b32de0c1349" :question/math-academy-id "q-40491" :question/problem "Let $Q$ be an orthogonal linear transformation. Which of the following are orthogonal sets?\n- **U.** $U={Q([\\begin{aligned}9 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}]),Q([\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}])}$\n- **W.** $W={Q([\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{6} \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]),Q([\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}])}$\n- **V.** $V={Q([\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]),Q([\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 4 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]),Q([\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}])}$\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Since $Q$ is orthogonal, we have that $Q(x)\\cdot Q(y)=x\\cdot y$ for any vectors $x$ and $y.$\n\nLet's denote the elements of $U,W,$ and $V$ respectively as \n\n$\\,$ ${Q({u}_{1}),Q({u}_{2})},\\,$ ${Q({w}_{1}),Q({w}_{2})},\\,$ and $\\,{Q({v}_{1}),Q({v}_{2}),Q({v}_{3})}.$ \n\nThe sets $U,V,$ and $W$ are orthogonal if the following conditions on each set hold:\n\n- $Q({u}_{1})\\cdot Q({u}_{2})=0.$\n\n- $Q({w}_{1})\\cdot Q({w}_{2})=0.$\n\n- $Q({v}_{1})\\cdot Q({v}_{2})=0,\\,Q({v}_{1})\\cdot Q({v}_{3})=0,\\,Q({v}_{2})\\cdot Q({v}_{3})=0.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our sets in turn.\n\n- For the set $U,$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q({u}_{1})\\cdot Q({u}_{2}) & ={u}_{1}\\cdot {u}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}9 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]=18-18=0.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the set $U$ is orthogonal.\n\n- For the set $W,$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q({w}_{1})\\cdot Q({w}_{2}) & ={w}_{1}\\cdot {w}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{6} \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]=1-1=0.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the set $W$ is orthogonal.\n\n- For the Set $V,$ notice that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q({v}_{1})\\cdot Q({v}_{2}) & ={v}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 4 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]=2+12-5=9\\ne 0.\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $V$ is not an orthogonal set.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$U$ and $W$ only\"."} :step/id #uuid "5c0620be-2d88-50a9-b295-5b90712b0a8b" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186406659}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186406659 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186406654 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "24e42699-a0f0-5e81-a194-c91514b7e8ae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406638} {:answer/id #uuid "46c27bd0-b639-51d1-a04d-8098da45991e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406642} {:answer/id #uuid "b3565871-fc75-5a3f-bb6e-506cbb0458f5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406644} {:answer/id #uuid "bfd57b0f-3965-5b70-833f-7fa36e06ae4f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406646} {:answer/id #uuid "e14e0ad1-a7b2-5023-b79c-bff39fbd35df" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "6\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406649}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186406644} :answer-field/id #uuid "491c6e1c-81dd-5170-b618-833ce2679d93" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406651}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "8a69da2c-f673-5e64-a265-9fe904a3c9ad" :question/math-academy-id "q-40514" :question/problem "Let $Q$ be an orthogonal linear transformation. Find $‖Q(x)‖$, where $x=[\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{6} \\\\ 1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}].$" :question/worked-solution "Since $Q$ is orthogonal, we have that $Q(x)\\cdot Q(y)=x\\cdot y$ for any vectors $x$ and $y.$\n\nThe linear transformation $Q$ being orthogonal is equivalent to the following:\n\n- The matrix $Q$ of transformation $Q$ relative to any orthonormal basis (including the standard basis) of ${R}^{n}$ is orthogonal.\n\n- $||Q(x)||=||x||.$\n\n- If $x⊥y$ then $Q(x)⊥Q(y)$ for any $x,y\\in {R}^{n}.$ In general, $Q$ preserves the angle between any two vectors.\n\n- $Q$ maps any orthogonal (orthonormal) set into an orthogonal (orthonormal) set.\n\nIn this case, since $Q$ is orthogonal, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖Q(x)‖ & =‖x‖ \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{x}_{1}^{2}+{x}_{2}^{2}+{x}_{3}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{(\\sqrt{6})}^{2}+{1}^{2}+{3}^{2}} \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "c999b8fd-0d25-5711-adf5-8e3f0c95ef68"}] :activity/title "Orthogonal Linear Transformations" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406635}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046660} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186415158} :activity/id #uuid "e4410405-361b-5f52-bbe2-fae1dae52eb7" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050910 :topic/id #uuid "708a8959-ec54-51e9-bf31-27b3539bdb74" :topic/math-academy-id 4319} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186415157 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284748 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5a4ade3a-303a-5eec-9df3-e43d050e7127" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186465651} {:answer/id #uuid "bfb5336b-989f-5a31-b653-6429b31a077f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186465652}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186465651} :answer-field/id #uuid "50d64a07-1676-5fda-bef0-6a4849be81d2" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466669} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6722f30e-e2af-5a70-879f-cfff27425f84" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186465653} {:answer/id #uuid "dc3d0a9a-fffb-56ce-a7bd-59c663e5f87e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186465654}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186465653} :answer-field/id #uuid "c9cb2af9-89d8-5f3d-8d83-90156885a877" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466670} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9ff5d22d-d9d1-5f3d-acef-b6be4a77c7ab" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186465655} {:answer/id #uuid "bb044ed9-c0d7-5063-b76d-362678932554" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186465656}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186465655} :answer-field/id #uuid "3cba7c41-2ad1-5047-8e7d-0cc187a92400" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466671}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "1e67e934-1049-5a47-aaca-94c1d2a3bfc1" :question/math-academy-id "q-177118" :question/problem "$$\n{u}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}],\\,{u}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 4 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}],\\,{u}_{3}=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the vectors above, and let $Q$ be an orthogonal linear transformation. Which of the following statements are true?\n- **I.** ${u}_{1}\\cdot {u}_{2}=0$\n- **II.** ${Q({u}_{1}),Q({u}_{2}),Q({u}_{3})}$ is orthogonal\n- **III.** ${Q({u}_{1}),Q({u}_{2}),Q({u}_{3})}$ is orthonormal\n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked." :question/worked-solution "Since $Q$ is orthogonal, we have that $Q(x)\\cdot Q(y)=x\\cdot y$ for any vectors $x$ and $y.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statement I is false since\n\n$$\n{u}_{1}\\cdot {u}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 4 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]=2+12-5=9\\ne 0.\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. Notice that, for example, $Q({u}_{1})⊥̸Q({u}_{2})$ since\n\n$$\nQ({u}_{1})\\cdot Q({u}_{2})={u}_{1}\\cdot {u}_{2}\\ne 0.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false since the set is not even orthogonal.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"None of the statements are true.\""} :step/id #uuid "2a7a4301-aad4-5d08-a205-223ae4b21b05" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186415161}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186415158 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198749 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e6dfedbf-e8f5-5c35-ac7a-3280352e020a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459653} {:answer/id #uuid "e9713a4a-6827-5ab4-8250-e0c6662fd257" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459654}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186459654} :answer-field/id #uuid "a72b8fb7-3248-5357-886c-14a9921e2b27" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186460693} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "37b51687-e975-5fef-b586-123a8d03457c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459655} {:answer/id #uuid "4719370a-40fe-5819-95db-2750f3e9f8ae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459656}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186459656} :answer-field/id #uuid "8a2ed651-b0b2-5578-8e7f-41bdc35291ec" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186460694} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "cb6b8dbf-9094-5908-9192-5e790c2e9227" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459657} {:answer/id #uuid "429f461b-d81f-54c3-bf90-863669545081" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459658}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186459657} :answer-field/id #uuid "5fb97e7e-61b1-52f5-b06e-85a2025b70ce" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186460695}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "70a4471c-4be9-5243-a617-e32ac5259d39" :question/math-academy-id "q-177075" :question/problem "$$\n{u}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}9 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}],\\,{u}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the vectors above, and let $Q$ be an orthogonal linear transformation. Which of the following statements are true?\n- **I.** ${u}_{1}\\cdot {u}_{2}=0$\n- **II.** ${Q({u}_{1}),Q({u}_{2})}$ is orthogonal\n- **III.** ${Q({u}_{1}),Q({u}_{2})}$ is orthonormal\n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked." :question/worked-solution "Since $Q$ is orthogonal, we have that $Q(x)\\cdot Q(y)=x\\cdot y$ for any vectors $x$ and $y.$\n\nThe linear transformation $Q$ being orthogonal is equivalent to the following:\n\n- The matrix $Q$ of transformation $Q$ relative to any orthonormal basis (including the standard basis) of ${R}^{n}$ is orthogonal.\n\n- $||Q(x)||=||x||.$\n\n- If $x⊥y$ then $Q(x)⊥Q(y)$ for any $x,y\\in {R}^{n}.$ In general, $Q$ preserves the angle between any two vectors.\n\n- $Q$ maps any orthogonal (orthonormal) set into an orthogonal (orthonormal) set.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed,\n\n$$\n{u}_{1}\\cdot {u}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}9 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]=18-18=0.\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, $Q({u}_{1})⊥Q({u}_{2})$ since\n\n$$\nQ({u}_{1})\\cdot Q({u}_{2})={u}_{1}\\cdot {u}_{2}=0.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. Notice that, for example, $Q({u}_{1})$ is not a unit vector since\n\n$$\n‖Q({u}_{1})‖=‖{u}_{1}‖=\\sqrt{{9}^{2}+{6}^{2}}\\ne 1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""} :step/id #uuid "721b390a-ee2e-5f0b-9e0f-16ccdf1c5e8b" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186415157}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186415159 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284759 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2a72e896-d047-