& =0\\cdot (-7)+1\\cdot (-5)+1\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =-4.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "81b7f578-5735-5cd8-9573-a001cb417b38" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186415160}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186415160 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186415156 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "81d08864-6101-5251-8351-5c0043998d07" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472778} {:answer/id #uuid "1a22dbe5-0d2b-5c0a-9531-8c54bafdc81e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472779}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186472779} :answer-field/id #uuid "71e4259d-b2ae-5c52-927d-7cf47899f776" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473784} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c84ccb50-f1be-591e-8bac-35014caba949" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472780} {:answer/id #uuid "19621db5-93e2-50ee-9ddb-8440f2d6f313" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472781}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186472781} :answer-field/id #uuid "7af95f9f-6948-54ed-b3d3-37b3b7e7a68c" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473785} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5b309452-f7e7-54e9-bbdf-9a3025bd85c0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472782} {:answer/id #uuid "f4968354-f03a-5157-9f0d-8be8dbd50643" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472783}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186472782} :answer-field/id #uuid "46245871-924f-5edd-8bb1-a6c28bc755b4" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473786}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "2a7fbace-2464-5d79-be05-e6f28745c3de" :question/math-academy-id "q-178014" :question/problem "$$\n{u}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -6\\end{aligned}],\\,{u}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the vectors above, and let $Q$ be an orthogonal linear transformation. Which of the following statements are true?\n- **I.** ${u}_{1}\\cdot {u}_{2}=0$\n- **II.** ${Q({u}_{1}),Q({u}_{2})}$ is orthogonal\n- **III.** ${Q({u}_{1}),Q({u}_{2})}$ is orthonormal\n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked." :question/worked-solution "Since $Q$ is orthogonal, we have that $Q(x)\\cdot Q(y)=x\\cdot y$ for any vectors $x$ and $y.$\n\nThe linear transformation $Q$ being orthogonal is equivalent to the following:\n\n- The matrix $Q$ of transformation $Q$ relative to any orthonormal basis (including the standard basis) of ${R}^{n}$ is orthogonal.\n\n- $||Q(x)||=||x||.$\n\n- If $x⊥y$ then $Q(x)⊥Q(y)$ for any $x,y\\in {R}^{n}.$ In general, $Q$ preserves the angle between any two vectors.\n\n- $Q$ maps any orthogonal (orthonormal) set into an orthogonal (orthonormal) set.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed,\n\n$$\n{u}_{1}\\cdot {u}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -6\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]=12-12=0.\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, $Q({u}_{1})⊥Q({u}_{2})$ since\n\n$$\nQ({u}_{1})\\cdot Q({u}_{2})={u}_{1}\\cdot {u}_{2}=0.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. Notice that, for example, $Q({u}_{1})$ is not a unit vector since\n\n$$\n‖Q({u}_{1})‖=‖{u}_{1}‖=\\sqrt{{3}^{2}+(-6)^{2}}\\ne 1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""} :step/id #uuid "99ecfa79-f64e-5756-8068-d2651506c9d0"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186415161 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284757 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "00a2dbbe-8269-5871-944e-208bd7aa03cb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281693} {:answer/id #uuid "5763789f-ad02-57d2-8abb-2d3ef223624f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4\\sqrt{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282431} {:answer/id #uuid "5f0011b6-dd86-54dd-93e2-86e2608cd8f1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282487} {:answer/id #uuid "aa73376a-4ff1-5a4a-8b18-a94ebfa6fe03" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "7\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283167} {:answer/id #uuid "f0a08606-13ff-5fb7-8e16-1af3bdc83744" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283789}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186282487} :answer-field/id #uuid "4e6e1327-d962-5a5a-86df-5a6da163058c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186284063}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "eeb4fa8d-201a-5a9d-8ffa-ae311083bf29" :question/math-academy-id "q-177769" :question/problem "Let $Q$ be an orthogonal linear transformation. Find $‖Q(x)‖$, where $x=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 3 \\\\ \\sqrt{6}\\end{aligned}].$" :question/worked-solution "Since $Q$ is orthogonal, we have that $Q(x)\\cdot Q(y)=x\\cdot y$ for any vectors $x$ and $y.$\n\nThe linear transformation $Q$ being orthogonal is equivalent to the following:\n\n- The matrix $Q$ of transformation $Q$ relative to any orthonormal basis (including the standard basis) of ${R}^{n}$ is orthogonal.\n\n- $||Q(x)||=||x||.$\n\n- If $x⊥y$ then $Q(x)⊥Q(y)$ for any $x,y\\in {R}^{n}.$ In general, $Q$ preserves the angle between any two vectors.\n\n- $Q$ maps any orthogonal (orthonormal) set into an orthogonal (orthonormal) set.\n\nIn this case, since $Q$ is orthogonal, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖Q(x)‖ & =‖x‖ \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{x}_{1}^{2}+{x}_{2}^{2}+{x}_{3}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-1)^{2}+{3}^{2}+{(\\sqrt{6})}^{2}} \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "ff6d9303-29e4-5651-9e3f-2878730378dc" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186415159}}] :activity/title "Orthogonal Linear Transformations" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415149}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046660} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186446172} :activity/id #uuid "33e49b6c-0f59-53bc-8c69-4b46de238ff6" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050910 :topic/id #uuid "708a8959-ec54-51e9-bf31-27b3539bdb74" :topic/math-academy-id 4319} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186446170 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186446168 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "85bffa49-25ed-5277-8eb0-2bf0d83a3acf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446158} {:answer/id #uuid "acfdb839-bbb9-5d75-a684-8e0ba2f1180c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "None of the statements are true." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446161} {:answer/id #uuid "e4112fbf-91d8-5bb1-acad-1777ebe798bc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446162} {:answer/id #uuid "eb713c79-d5f4-5d03-8da7-db12deacf3d2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446164} {:answer/id #uuid "f04b535e-e446-5693-b598-2a2ae8017998" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446165}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186446162} :answer-field/id #uuid "5243b6a3-407c-5377-9662-ad587ee4e1e3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446167}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "ac6fbe63-89e3-55f0-b383-00653c46559a" :question/math-academy-id "q-178025" :question/problem "$$\n{u}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ -\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}],\\,{u}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the vectors above, and let $Q$ be an orthogonal linear transformation. Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. ${u}_{1}\\cdot {u}_{2}=0$\n\nII. $\\{Q({u}_{1}),Q({u}_{2})\\}$ is orthogonal\n\nIII. $\\{Q({u}_{1}),Q({u}_{2})\\}$ is orthonormal" :question/worked-solution "Since $Q$ is orthogonal, we have that $Q(x)\\cdot Q(y)=x\\cdot y$ for any vectors $x$ and $y.$\n\nThe linear transformation $Q$ being orthogonal is equivalent to the following:\n\n- The matrix $Q$ of transformation $Q$ relative to any orthonormal basis (including the standard basis) of ${R}^{n}$ is orthogonal.\n\n- $||Q(x)||=||x||.$\n\n- If $x⊥y$ then $Q(x)⊥Q(y)$ for any $x,y\\in {R}^{n}.$ In general, $Q$ preserves the angle between any two vectors.\n\n- $Q$ maps any orthogonal (orthonormal) set into an orthogonal (orthonormal) set.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed,\n\n$$\n{u}_{1}\\cdot {u}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ -\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}]=\\frac{2}{5}-\\frac{2}{5}=0.\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, $Q({u}_{1})⊥Q({u}_{2})$ since\n\n$$\nQ({u}_{1})\\cdot Q({u}_{2})={u}_{1}\\cdot {u}_{2}=0.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, both $Q({u}_{1})$ and $Q({u}_{2})$ are unit vectors:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖Q({u}_{1})‖ & =‖{u}_{1}‖=\\sqrt{(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}})^{2}+(-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}})^{2}}=1 \\\\ ‖Q({u}_{2})‖ & =‖{u}_{2}‖=\\sqrt{(\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}})^{2}+(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}})^{2}}=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\""} :step/id #uuid "024575a8-c8f0-5c99-9c1d-bf6941663ece" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186446171}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186446171 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284764 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e1f6862c-27fd-5c39-8eb2-3cbbffed6df8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186465669} {:answer/id #uuid "786a7ab2-a3ec-5b80-8a00-e9e1753b87d4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186465670}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186465669} :answer-field/id #uuid "55fa8074-b199-52f0-b9ec-a64e6d2e74dd" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466678} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "14cbd69a-f982-5d8b-a7b5-faa1d54ef043" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186465671} {:answer/id #uuid "a3dbd501-b3c0-564f-92ea-a9a572fb9e89" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186465672}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186465671} :answer-field/id #uuid "77082866-697e-5473-bb08-593811756def" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466679} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "270b69ea-ae43-51a2-bc71-ac5760e7b855" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186465673} {:answer/id #uuid "f89e42f6-9f2a-5d60-9603-c8642f276749" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186465674}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186465673} :answer-field/id #uuid "07d18537-0a6d-59ae-9629-b8bdfac0f081" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466680}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "e16f6fa7-ae80-51bb-bdf3-1add916dcb13" :question/math-academy-id "q-178269" :question/problem "$$\n{u}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}],\\,{u}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the vectors above, and let $Q$ be an orthogonal linear transformation. Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. ${u}_{1}\\cdot {u}_{2}=0$\n\nII. $\\{Q({u}_{1}),Q({u}_{2})\\}$ is orthogonal\n\nIII. $\\{Q({u}_{1}),Q({u}_{2})\\}$ is orthonormal\n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked." :question/worked-solution "Since $Q$ is orthogonal, we have that $Q(x)\\cdot Q(y)=x\\cdot y$ for any vectors $x$ and $y.$\n\nThe linear transformation $Q$ being orthogonal is equivalent to the following:\n\n- The matrix $Q$ of transformation $Q$ relative to any orthonormal basis (including the standard basis) of ${R}^{n}$ is orthogonal.\n\n- $||Q(x)||=||x||.$\n\n- If $x⊥y$ then $Q(x)⊥Q(y)$ for any $x,y\\in {R}^{n}.$ In general, $Q$ preserves the angle between any two vectors.\n\n- $Q$ maps any orthogonal (orthonormal) set into an orthogonal (orthonormal) set.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statement I is false. Indeed,\n\n$$\n{u}_{1}\\cdot {u}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]=6+3\\ne 0.\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. Notice that, $Q({u}_{1})⊥̸Q({u}_{2})$ since\n\n$$\nQ({u}_{1})\\cdot Q({u}_{2})={u}_{1}\\cdot {u}_{2}\\ne 0.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false since the set is not even orthogonal.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"None of the statements are true.\""} :step/id #uuid "04e86d6f-be2b-5bc9-ac7f-9c117105b2fe"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186446172 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198978 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c5739013-0ec3-5054-9df2-0416169aee06" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186197739} {:answer/id #uuid "cb80e9c9-9a83-5c04-a3ab-b7735d5f125a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "5\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186197740} {:answer/id #uuid "cc77a078-56f6-5c64-a1d0-37853dbff1ba" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186197741} {:answer/id #uuid "e099e132-6017-54bd-af9b-dabda71bcfb4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186197742} {:answer/id #uuid "2c36f56b-9a3f-5112-a631-00ca6bd5658b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186197743}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186197742} :answer-field/id #uuid "ec57d5bb-fbdf-5711-9938-796ddd4e592a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186197738}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "5b4e4425-10cb-5811-9da8-6b08b3f0fd92" :question/math-academy-id "q-40489" :question/problem "Let $Q$ be an orthogonal linear transformation. Find $‖Q(x)‖$, where $x=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 4 \\\\ \\sqrt{5}\\end{aligned}].$" :question/worked-solution "Since $Q$ is orthogonal, we have that $Q(x)\\cdot Q(y)=x\\cdot y$ for any vectors $x$ and $y.$\n\nThe linear transformation $Q$ being orthogonal is equivalent to the following:\n\n- The matrix $Q$ of transformation $Q$ relative to any orthonormal basis (including the standard basis) of ${R}^{n}$ is orthogonal.\n\n- $||Q(x)||=||x||.$\n\n- If $x⊥y$ then $Q(x)⊥Q(y)$ for any $x,y\\in {R}^{n}.$ In general, $Q$ preserves the angle between any two vectors.\n\n- $Q$ maps any orthogonal (orthonormal) set into an orthogonal (orthonormal) set.\n\nIn this case, since $Q$ is orthogonal, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖Q(x)‖ & =‖x‖ \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{x}_{1}^{2}+{x}_{2}^{2}+{x}_{3}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{2}^{2}+{4}^{2}+{(\\sqrt{5})}^{2}} \\\\  & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "198647b9-7961-5263-912d-818d3e048db4" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186446173}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186446173 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186406654 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "24e42699-a0f0-5e81-a194-c91514b7e8ae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406638} {:answer/id #uuid "46c27bd0-b639-51d1-a04d-8098da45991e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406642} {:answer/id #uuid "b3565871-fc75-5a3f-bb6e-506cbb0458f5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406644} {:answer/id #uuid "bfd57b0f-3965-5b70-833f-7fa36e06ae4f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406646} {:answer/id #uuid "e14e0ad1-a7b2-5023-b79c-bff39fbd35df" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "6\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406649}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186406644} :answer-field/id #uuid "491c6e1c-81dd-5170-b618-833ce2679d93" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406651}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "8a69da2c-f673-5e64-a265-9fe904a3c9ad" :question/math-academy-id "q-40514" :question/problem "Let $Q$ be an orthogonal linear transformation. Find $‖Q(x)‖$, where $x=[\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{6} \\\\ 1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}].$" :question/worked-solution "Since $Q$ is orthogonal, we have that $Q(x)\\cdot Q(y)=x\\cdot y$ for any vectors $x$ and $y.$\n\nThe linear transformation $Q$ being orthogonal is equivalent to the following:\n\n- The matrix $Q$ of transformation $Q$ relative to any orthonormal basis (including the standard basis) of ${R}^{n}$ is orthogonal.\n\n- $||Q(x)||=||x||.$\n\n- If $x⊥y$ then $Q(x)⊥Q(y)$ for any $x,y\\in {R}^{n}.$ In general, $Q$ preserves the angle between any two vectors.\n\n- $Q$ maps any orthogonal (orthonormal) set into an orthogonal (orthonormal) set.\n\nIn this case, since $Q$ is orthogonal, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖Q(x)‖ & =‖x‖ \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{x}_{1}^{2}+{x}_{2}^{2}+{x}_{3}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{(\\sqrt{6})}^{2}+{1}^{2}+{3}^{2}} \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "3d1ac785-35a7-523f-9e93-3c458c7662c9" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186446174}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186446174 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186446169 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1bd4d334-2269-5bfe-a24c-a58aafbef588" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446156} {:answer/id #uuid "4a46d5a3-3c48-5050-85ff-c52a5b9baaee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446157} {:answer/id #uuid "9164dcc8-fbe9-5d34-a2f1-c8be97a45615" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446159} {:answer/id #uuid "a8c1505c-5ad9-511e-a22d-30a3286bfbdb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446160} {:answer/id #uuid "e705ebf2-c558-54c0-816f-602c5af2facd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446163}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186446163} :answer-field/id #uuid "45554d58-8407-5972-941e-77d5ad8113b4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446166}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "bffc4e85-37a7-58e6-9707-94d9ac87c744" :question/math-academy-id "q-177893" :question/problem "Let $Q$ be an orthogonal linear transformation. Find $Q(x)\\cdot Q(y)$, where $x=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$ and $y=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].$" :question/worked-solution "Since $Q$ is orthogonal, we have that $Q(x)\\cdot Q(y)=x\\cdot y$ for any vectors $x$ and $y.$\n\nThe linear transformation $Q$ being orthogonal is equivalent to the following:\n\n- The matrix $Q$ of transformation $Q$ relative to any orthonormal basis (including the standard basis) of ${R}^{n}$ is orthogonal.\n\n- $||Q(x)||=||x||.$\n\n- If $x⊥y$ then $Q(x)⊥Q(y)$ for any $x,y\\in {R}^{n}.$ In general, $Q$ preserves the angle between any two vectors.\n\n- $Q$ maps any orthogonal (orthonormal) set into an orthogonal (orthonormal) set.\n\nIn this case, since $Q$ is orthogonal, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(x)\\cdot Q(y) & =x\\cdot y \\\\  & =3\\cdot 2+1\\cdot 2+1\\cdot 0 \\\\  & =8.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "87144feb-48ca-5cf7-a2fa-4efd26384df9" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186446170}}] :activity/title "Orthogonal Linear Transformations" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446155}] [{:activity/expected-seconds 660.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134636} :activity/id #uuid "600046de-0665-50c3-a999-1a23cc41313b" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050993 :topic/id #uuid "77b8e530-6866-509a-8397-d35f1b4f5874" :topic/math-academy-id 2097} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134636 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186135034 :tutorial/content "The standard inner product of $\\Bbb R^n,$ i.e. the dot product, does not meet the criteria of an inner product on $\\Bbb C^n.$\n\nFor example, if $\\langle \\,\\cdot\\,, \\,\\cdot\\, \\rangle$ is a valid inner product on $\\Bbb C^n,$ then for any vector $\\mathbf a \\in \\Bbb C^n,$ we must have\n\n$$\n\\langle\\mathbf a, \\, \\mathbf a\\rangle \\ge 0.\n$$\n\nBut for many vectors in $\\Bbb C^n,$ such as $[\\begin{aligned}\\text{i} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$ in $\\Bbb C^2,$ the dot product fails to satisfy this criterion:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}\\text{i} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}\\text{i} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] & ={\\text{i}}^{2}+{0}^{2} \\\\  & =-1 \\\\  & <0\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThankfully, there is an elegant way to generalize the dot product to satisfy the desired criterion on $\\Bbb C^n.$ We can just take the complex conjugate of each element in the right vector before carrying out the dot product.\n\nMore formally, for two vectors $\\mathbf x, \\mathbf y\\in \\mathbb C^n$ given by\n\n![](images/26/26b805ac1354eab1901a0874f66882bc1ec07dc3351c95ad2cf9b16aaf59d2fb.svg)\n\nwe define the **standard Hermitian inner product** as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨x,y⟩ & ={x}_{1}\\cdot \\overset{―}{{y}_{1}}+{x}_{2}\\cdot \\overset{―}{{y}_{2}}+⋯+{x}_{n}\\cdot \\overset{―}{{y}_{n}} \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}\\cdot \\overset{―}{{y}_{i}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $\\overline{y_i}$ is the complex conjugate of $y_i.$\n\nUsing the standard Hermitian inner product instead of the dot product, our desired criterion is satisfied:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨[\\begin{aligned}\\text{i} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}\\text{i} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]⟩ & =\\text{i}\\cdot \\overset{―}{\\,\\text{i}\\,}+0\\cdot \\overset{―}{\\,0\\,} \\\\  & =\\text{i}\\cdot (-\\text{i})+0\\cdot 0 \\\\  & =1+0 \\\\  & =1 \\\\  & \\ge 0\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIt can be verified that the Hermitian inner product satisfies *all* the criteria of an inner product on $\\Bbb C^n.$\n\nFurthermore, since the complex conjugate leaves real numbers unchanged, the Hermitian inner product simplifies to the dot product on $\\Bbb R^n.$ In other words, the dot product is just a special case of the Hermitian inner product!" :tutorial/id #uuid "ca70c748-2628-5d2d-b0dc-ab7a0675355a" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3605 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "5959b5ca-9405-5f0f-b4d5-5a7a8e442cfc" :step/math-academy-id 20193 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134637}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134637 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133765 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133574 :question/id #uuid "3263f466-0901-58a7-8cd9-ed62cd3036a3" :question/math-academy-id "e-5545"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "85ae774f-2496-56c0-a800-450bd8383092" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048169} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048701} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048861}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134424} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134425} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272190} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329732} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329733} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329734} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329735} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329739} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338874} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360718} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186411853}] :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Inner Product of Two Complex Vectors"} :step/id #uuid "1f2f6fb1-f106-52c7-b55a-201253d4ed07" :step/math-academy-id 20194 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134804}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134638 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133766 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133575 :question/id #uuid "b30bd921-7418-5a57-96eb-0c79dd5e4712" :question/math-academy-id "e-5547"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "42258cf7-c1cf-5cc3-876d-102cc2233083" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134551} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134552} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272226} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272227} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360759} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360772} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360773} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360774} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360775} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360783} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360784} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360785}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Regarding the Hermitian Inner Product"} :step/id #uuid "f393944b-d78a-5d8a-aa1a-c7c5133f9717" :step/math-academy-id 20196 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134805}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134804 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186135100 :tutorial/content "A Hermitian inner product over $\\mathbb{C}$ satisfies the following properties:\n\n- It is **linear in the first argument.** For any $\\mathbf{a}, \\mathbf{b}, \\mathbf{c} \\in \\mathbb{C}^n$ and any $\\alpha \\in \\mathbb{C},$ we have \n\n$\\qquad$ $\\langle \\mathbf{a} + \\mathbf{b}, \\mathbf{c} \\rangle = \\langle \\mathbf{a}, \\mathbf{c} \\rangle + \\langle \\mathbf{b}, \\mathbf{c} \\rangle \\quad$ and $\\quad \\langle \\alpha \\mathbf{a}, \\mathbf{b} \\rangle = \\alpha \\langle \\mathbf{a}, \\mathbf{b} \\rangle.$\n\n- It is **antilinear in the second argument.** For any $\\mathbf{a}, \\mathbf{b}, \\mathbf{c} \\in \\mathbb{C}^n$ and any $\\alpha \\in \\mathbb{C},$ we have\n\n$\\qquad$ $\\langle \\mathbf{a}, \\mathbf{b} + \\mathbf{c} \\rangle = \\langle \\mathbf{a}, \\mathbf{b} \\rangle + \\langle \\mathbf{a}, \\mathbf{c} \\rangle \\quad$ and $\\quad \\langle \\mathbf{a}, \\alpha\\mathbf{b} \\rangle = \\overline{\\alpha} \\langle \\mathbf{a}, \\mathbf{b} \\rangle.$\n\n- It has **conjugate symmetry.** For any $\\mathbf{a}, \\mathbf{b} \\in \\mathbb{C}^n,$ we have\n\n$\\qquad$ $\\langle \\mathbf{a}, \\mathbf{b} \\rangle = \\overline{\\langle \\mathbf{b}, \\mathbf{a} \\rangle}.$\n\n- It is **positive-definite.** For any $\\mathbf{a}\\in \\mathbb{C}^n,$ we have\n\n$\\qquad$ $\\langle \\mathbf{a}, \\mathbf{a} \\rangle$ is a non-negative real number, and $\\langle \\mathbf{a}, \\mathbf{a} \\rangle = 0$ if and only if $\\mathbf{a} = \\mathbf{0}.$" :tutorial/id #uuid "44cf93d3-a069-54ee-bfbf-93d36fd338cb" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5088 :tutorial/title "Properties of the Inner Product Over the Complex Numbers"} :step/id #uuid "f16cfe03-2cd2-5567-81c0-5f200f0b4b5f" :step/math-academy-id 31867 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134638}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134805 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133865 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133681 :question/id #uuid "8d5f83f0-dfb9-5437-a0e5-f33f68a59cec" :question/math-academy-id "e-8578"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d59ac953-c511-5d6f-902d-4aeb804c99ce" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134549} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134550} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272228} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272229} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360761} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360762} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360765} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360766} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360767} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360769} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360770} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360771}] :knowledge-point/title "Applying Properties of the Hermitian Inner Product"} :step/id #uuid "418336f6-44aa-5629-a383-cc4e0867f9c9" :step/math-academy-id 31868}] :activity/title "The Inner Product in Vector Spaces Over the Complex Numbers" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186133984}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046660} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186445245} :activity/id #uuid "871d2b00-341d-5ec3-92a3-8fd5aa7eca28" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050993 :topic/id #uuid "77b8e530-6866-509a-8397-d35f1b4f5874" :topic/math-academy-id 2097} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186445241 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360774 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "914c5cc0-77de-5e5a-9430-0c8663b7fb16" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452347} {:answer/id #uuid "7e5b3124-7a98-5669-a577-2b30cc503898" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452348}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186452348} :answer-field/id #uuid "0dc138d2-4f7c-59f8-a707-746570e3a3b8" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453021} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5971c655-545f-582e-9080-64ecfc5176a0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452349} {:answer/id #uuid "aae144d6-fa3b-515c-a5b3-99f9a33f4896" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452350}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186452349} :answer-field/id #uuid "5bcc5079-bb62-5377-8955-c42eea6e68ea" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453022} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0e5ada01-d7b5-56ef-a0ba-92bb91518486" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452351} {:answer/id #uuid "83a11f7d-c0f2-5e90-97d1-c0f4da2784c6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452352}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186452351} :answer-field/id #uuid "b94f1c25-e6f5-54ad-badc-528ff35eb492" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453023}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "2fac0a4e-d3e8-507c-b2ff-dcb288cf0e43" :question/math-academy-id "q-97258" :question/problem "Consider the standard Hermitian inner product $⟨\\,\\cdot \\,,\\,\\cdot \\,⟩$ over $C.$ \nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $⟨a,a⟩\\in R$ for any $a\\in {C}^{n}$\n\n- **II.** $⟨a,\\alpha b⟩=\\alpha ⟨a,b⟩$ for any $a,b\\in {C}^{n}$ and any $\\alpha \\in C$\n\n- **III.** $⟨a,b⟩=⟨b,a⟩$ for any $a,b\\in {C}^{n}$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the standard Hermitian inner product over $C$ satisfies the following properties:\n\n- It is linear in the first argument. For any $a,b,c\\in {C}^{n}$ and any $\\alpha \\in C,$ we have\n\n$\\,$ $⟨a+b,c⟩=⟨a,c⟩+⟨b,c⟩\\,$ and $\\,⟨\\alpha a,b⟩=\\alpha ⟨a,b⟩.$\n\n- It is antilinear in the second argument. For any $a,b,c\\in {C}^{n}$ and any $\\alpha \\in C,$ we have\n\n$\\,$ $⟨a,b+c⟩=⟨a,b⟩+⟨a,c⟩\\,$ and $\\,⟨a,\\alpha b⟩=\\overset{―}{\\alpha }⟨a,b⟩.$\n\n- It has conjugate symmetry. For any $a,b\\in {C}^{n},$ we have\n\n$\\,$ $⟨a,b⟩=\\overset{―}{⟨b,a⟩}.$\n\n- It is positive-definite. For any $a\\in {C}^{n},$ we have\n\n$\\,$ $⟨a,a⟩$ is a non-negative real number, and $⟨a,a⟩=0$ if and only if $a=0.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements. \n\n- Statement I is true. For any $a\\in {C}^{n},$  $⟨a,a⟩$ is a non-negative real number.\n\n- Statement II is false. To factor out a constant multiple from the second argument, we must change it to its complex conjugate.\n\n- Statement III is false. The inner product over $C$ has conjugate symmetry.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""} :step/id #uuid "042f271a-62ae-5a4a-884d-ff79b9db9024" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186445244}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186445242 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360767 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0e841129-5370-59a4-a247-981a63a55e7e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357872} {:answer/id #uuid "0f6fe134-1090-5f97-9ea8-128ddfc3b774" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357876} {:answer/id #uuid "c020effb-ac1b-5c1a-b136-a3f990641cda" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-2\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359435} {:answer/id #uuid "cb7d8484-175b-5e8d-bc3b-f162579d8e47" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359541} {:answer/id #uuid "e819a706-cc55-53d4-ad1b-913fba1a9b02" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-5+3\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359809}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186359541} :answer-field/id #uuid "a14982e8-8062-5f22-9abd-32b12535e617" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360273}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "34140471-4f5c-5a2d-9e46-e76840a3d73f" :question/math-academy-id "q-97193" :question/problem "Given that $⟨\\,\\cdot \\,,\\,\\cdot \\,⟩$ is the standard Hermitian inner product over $C,$ find $⟨a,\\,(4-\\text{i})b+c⟩$ if  \n\n$\\,$ $⟨b,a⟩=-1-\\text{i}\\,$ and $\\,⟨a,c⟩=5.$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the inner product over $C$ satisfies the following properties. \n\n- It is linear in the first argument. For any $a,b,c\\in {C}^{n}$ and any $\\alpha \\in C,$ we have\n\n$\\,$ $⟨a+b,c⟩=⟨a,c⟩+⟨b,c⟩\\,$ and $\\,⟨\\alpha a,b⟩=\\alpha ⟨a,b⟩.$\n\n- It is antilinear in the second argument. For any $a,b,c\\in {C}^{n}$ and any $\\alpha \\in C,$ we have\n\n$\\,$ $⟨a,b+c⟩=⟨a,b⟩+⟨a,c⟩\\,$ and $\\,⟨a,\\alpha b⟩=\\overset{―}{\\alpha }⟨a,b⟩.$\n\n- It has conjugate symmetry. For any $a,b\\in {C}^{n},$ we have\n\n$\\,$ $⟨a,b⟩=\\overset{―}{⟨b,a⟩}.$\n\n- It is positive-definite. For any $a\\in {C}^{n},$ we have\n\n$\\,$ $⟨a,a⟩$ is a non-negative real number, and $⟨a,a⟩=0$ if and only if $a=0.$\n\nFirst, we use the fact that the inner product over $C$ is antilinear in the second argument:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨a,\\,(4-\\text{i})b+c⟩ & =⟨a,(4-\\text{i})b⟩+⟨a,c⟩ \\\\  & =(\\overset{―}{4-\\text{i}})⟨a,b⟩+⟨a,c⟩ \\\\  & =(4+\\text{i})⟨a,b⟩+⟨a,c⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use the fact that the inner product over $C$ has conjugate symmetry:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(4+\\text{i})⟨a,b⟩+⟨a,c\\text{ }⟩ & =(4+\\text{i})\\overset{―}{⟨b,a⟩}+⟨a,c⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute the values for $⟨b,a⟩$ and $⟨a,c⟩:$  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(4+\\text{i})\\overset{―}{⟨b,a⟩}+⟨a,c⟩ & =(4+\\text{i})\\cdot (\\overset{―}{-1-\\text{i}})+5 \\\\  & =(4+\\text{i})\\cdot (-1+\\text{i})+5 \\\\  & =-5+3\\text{i}+5 \\\\  & =3\\text{i}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "22a3d38c-45ed-5261-9a9f-bdb8efdf26a5" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186445241}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186445243 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272227 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9fa21b8e-37bf-549c-a839-8ce08870e48c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449622} {:answer/id #uuid "1b6c92f3-d7f4-5664-9cea-aea62fa675cd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449623}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186449623} :answer-field/id #uuid "a6affe32-9119-5e97-99f7-cd7280f8fef2" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450765} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4a053bee-dd05-5cff-aa1d-6027f193cc6d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449624} {:answer/id #uuid "db9ce199-be8e-5342-98b1-98b5521bbdb6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449625}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186449625} :answer-field/id #uuid "d6da9d5b-43d3-5bf4-b853-80d6034a446d" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450766} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "42386a6f-47ee-5149-8998-cff1d0d60579" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449626} {:answer/id #uuid "c8170793-2b53-56a5-904e-e65bc454b18f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449627}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186449626} :answer-field/id #uuid "096d6ae4-f1ec-5de4-94a5-89d0daa3b32d" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450767}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "396a82c3-ba69-5d62-8d39-94fffd303517" :question/math-academy-id "q-141160" :question/problem "Consider the standard Hermitian inner product $⟨\\,\\cdot \\,,\\,\\cdot \\,⟩$ over $C.$ \nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $⟨ia,b⟩=i⟨a,b⟩$ for any $a,b\\in {C}^{n}$\n\n- **II.** $⟨a,\\overset{―}{\\alpha }b⟩=⟨\\alpha a,b⟩$ for any $a,b\\in {C}^{n}$ and any $\\alpha \\in C$\n\n- **III.** $⟨ia,a⟩\\in R$ for any $a\\in {C}^{n}$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the standard Hermitian inner product over $C$ satisfies the following properties:\n\n- It is linear in the first argument. For any $a,b,c\\in {C}^{n}$ and any $\\alpha \\in C,$ we have\n\n$\\,$ $⟨a+b,c⟩=⟨a,c⟩+⟨b,c⟩\\,$ and $\\,⟨\\alpha a,b⟩=\\alpha ⟨a,b⟩.$\n\n- It is antilinear in the second argument. For any $a,b,c\\in {C}^{n}$ and any $\\alpha \\in C,$ we have\n\n$\\,$ $⟨a,b+c⟩=⟨a,b⟩+⟨a,c⟩\\,$ and $\\,⟨a,\\alpha b⟩=\\overset{―}{\\alpha }⟨a,b⟩.$\n\n- It has conjugate symmetry. For any $a,b\\in {C}^{n},$ we have\n\n$\\,$ $⟨a,b⟩=\\overset{―}{⟨b,a⟩}.$\n\n- It is positive-definite. For any $a\\in {C}^{n},$ we have\n\n$\\,$ $⟨a,a⟩$ is a non-negative real number, and $⟨a,a⟩=0$ if and only if $a=0.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements. \n\n- Statement I is true.  Indeed, the inner product in ${C}^{n}$ is linear in the first argument. So, a constant multiple from the first argument can be factored out.\n\n- Statement II is true. By the antilinearity of a complex inner product in the second argument and linearity in the first argument, we have \n\n$$\n⟨a,\\overset{―}{\\alpha }b⟩=\\overset{―}{(\\overset{―}{\\alpha })}⟨a,b⟩=\\alpha ⟨a,b⟩=⟨\\alpha a,b⟩.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. Notice that $⟨a,a⟩\\in R$ for any $a\\in {C}^{n}.$ Therefore, if $a\\ne 0,$ then\n\n$$\n⟨ia,a⟩=i⟨a,a⟩\\notin R.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""} :step/id #uuid "4ce6b6ce-a0a5-5ded-b78a-9436ef265d55"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186445244 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360783 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9be7f699-9974-5201-8e47-accce63d5602" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452377} {:answer/id #uuid "19455cdc-333a-5561-bab6-00ec79218d8e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452378}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186452377} :answer-field/id #uuid "5582b199-c5bf-5709-9377-1286b2173eaa" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453036} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d491e753-0d53-5704-b768-2cde723d0d30" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452379} {:answer/id #uuid "fda07ff9-ba7b-5689-857e-ab9f1cd84952" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452380}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186452380} :answer-field/id #uuid "f3df9eb2-97cb-5280-9fe0-ea7eec6cc287" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453037} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8d1794a0-b828-5e06-b8cc-54a54ad72949" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452381} {:answer/id #uuid "b15deefa-87b8-5a6a-89b0-4f57df04b649" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452382}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186452381} :answer-field/id #uuid "1b86e18f-b4ac-528d-b5e3-1bc836241d7d" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453038}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "354bca31-ea8a-5e1d-a896-d3e34e677d11" :question/math-academy-id "q-97296" :question/problem "Consider the standard Hermitian inner product $⟨\\,\\cdot \\,,\\,\\cdot \\,⟩$ over $C.$ \nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $⟨a,b⟩=\\overset{―}{⟨a,b⟩}$ for any $a,b\\in {C}^{n}$\n\n- **II.** $i⟨a,b⟩=⟨a,-ib⟩$ for any  $a,b\\in {C}^{n}$\n\n- **III.** There is vector $a\\in {C}^{n}$ such that  $⟨a,a⟩<0$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the standard Hermitian inner product over $C$ satisfies the following properties:\n\n- It is linear in the first argument. For any $a,b,c\\in {C}^{n}$ and any $\\alpha \\in C,$ we have\n\n$\\,$ $⟨a+b,c⟩=⟨a,c⟩+⟨b,c⟩\\,$ and $\\,⟨\\alpha a,b⟩=\\alpha ⟨a,b⟩.$\n\n- It is antilinear in the second argument. For any $a,b,c\\in {C}^{n}$ and any $\\alpha \\in C,$ we have\n\n$\\,$ $⟨a,b+c⟩=⟨a,b⟩+⟨a,c⟩\\,$ and $\\,⟨a,\\alpha b⟩=\\overset{―}{\\alpha }⟨a,b⟩.$\n\n- It has conjugate symmetry. For any $a,b\\in {C}^{n},$ we have\n\n$\\,$ $⟨a,b⟩=\\overset{―}{⟨b,a⟩}.$\n\n- It is positive-definite. For any $a\\in {C}^{n},$ we have\n\n$\\,$ $⟨a,a⟩$ is a non-negative real number, and $⟨a,a⟩=0$ if and only if $a=0.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements. \n\n- Statement I is false. A complex number is equal to its conjugate if and only if it is a real number. The inner product over $C$  is a complex number in general.\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, by the antilinearity of a complex inner product in the second argument, we have \n\n$$\n⟨a,-ib⟩=\\overset{―}{(-i)}⟨a,b⟩=i⟨a,b⟩.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false.  The inner product over $C$ is positive-definite. So, for any $a\\in {C}^{n},$ we have\n$\\,$ $⟨a,a⟩\\ge 0.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""} :step/id #uuid "c0e9081f-8d78-5a6a-b192-5ac752a618f5" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186445243}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186445245 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134550 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "cda7ef33-fa7f-5bfa-b9a6-e5261e06d70c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382622} {:answer/id #uuid "944aaef0-c92b-5460-b4bf-078c629fb5ae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-2+3\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382623} {:answer/id #uuid "0a96dcaf-f1c7-527a-b08a-1d68a986f634" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382624} {:answer/id #uuid "27ad374f-3361-5475-8073-b762bb17dd5f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-1+5\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382625} {:answer/id #uuid "14945f42-7413-59f1-969e-cad3bb3baa75" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "5-2\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382626}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186382625} :answer-field/id #uuid "fbcb91a3-9ab8-5494-a94e-36070be9094c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382621}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "8f2e5445-afeb-5118-844a-129faadbb234" :question/math-academy-id "q-96821" :question/problem "Given that $⟨\\,\\cdot \\,,\\,\\cdot \\,⟩$ is the standard Hermitian inner product over $C,$ find $⟨a,\\,b-(1+3\\text{i})c⟩$ if \n\n$\\,$ $⟨b,a⟩=1+\\text{i}\\,$ and $\\,⟨a,c⟩=2.$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the inner product over $C$ satisfies the following properties. \n\n- It is linear in the first argument. For any $a,b,c\\in {C}^{n}$ and any $\\alpha \\in C,$ we have\n\n$\\,$ $⟨a+b,c⟩=⟨a,c⟩+⟨b,c⟩\\,$ and $\\,⟨\\alpha a,b⟩=\\alpha ⟨a,b⟩.$\n\n- It is antilinear in the second argument. For any $a,b,c\\in {C}^{n}$ and any $\\alpha \\in C,$ we have\n\n$\\,$ $⟨a,b+c⟩=⟨a,b⟩+⟨a,c⟩\\,$ and $\\,⟨a,\\alpha b⟩=\\overset{―}{\\alpha }⟨a,b⟩.$\n\n- It has conjugate symmetry. For any $a,b\\in {C}^{n},$ we have\n\n$\\,$ $⟨a,b⟩=\\overset{―}{⟨b,a⟩}.$\n\n- It is positive-definite. For any $a\\in {C}^{n},$ we have\n\n$\\,$ $⟨a,a⟩$ is a non-negative real number, and $⟨a,a⟩=0$ if and only if $a=0.$\n\nFirst, we use the fact that the inner product over $C$ is antilinear in the second argument:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨a,\\,b-(1+3\\text{i})c⟩ & =⟨a,b⟩-⟨a,(1+3\\text{i})c⟩ \\\\  & =⟨a,b⟩-(\\overset{―}{1+3\\text{i}})⟨a,c⟩ \\\\  & =⟨a,b⟩-(1-3\\text{i})⟨a,c⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use the fact that the inner product over $C$ has conjugate symmetry:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨a,b⟩-(1-3\\text{i})⟨a,c⟩ & =\\overset{―}{⟨b,a⟩}-(1-3\\text{i})⟨a,c⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute the values for $⟨b,a⟩$ and $⟨a,c⟩:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{―}{⟨b,a⟩}-(1-3\\text{i})⟨a,c⟩ & =\\overset{―}{1+\\text{i}}-(1-3\\text{i})\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =1-\\text{i}-2+6\\text{i} \\\\  & =-1+5\\text{i}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "ff45ad40-043f-5835-bb86-3d1e620fd852" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186445242}}] :activity/title "The Inner Product in Vector Spaces Over the Complex Numbers" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186445240}]}
